{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'mechanism draws findings|Small first|At least three|Write the decision|three samples|conjunction' ../../../AGENTS.md; rg -n 'pair|H1|H2|H4|Credit|Readable|Fail|Pass|Inconclusive|Price' PAID-DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^##|''^'\"\\\\*\\\\*' codex-r{22,23,24,25,26,27,28,29,30}.md; rg -n '\"'^##|acceptance|ledger|retire|mutant|run'\"' PAID-BUILD.md\"","aggregated_output":"codex-r30.md:1:**NOT READY.** The rebuild closes the round-29 rejected-summary and missing-packet paths. H3 still misses cases it is meant to guard, and I reproduced a pass with an incomplete gain review and a failure from duplicate H5 rows.\ncodex-r30.md:3:### Round-29 fixes that hold\ncodex-r30.md:7:### Blocking findings\ncodex-r30.md:12:### Reading time\ncodex-r30.md:16:**$7 remains a reasonable ceiling to propose once the blockers are fixed.**\ncodex-r29.md:1:**NOT READY.** The rebuild closes several round-28 gaps, but I reproduced two false passes: one using a sequence the acceptance gate rejected, and another with no completed harm or gain-judgement reviews.\ncodex-r29.md:3:### What holds as fixed\ncodex-r29.md:7:### Blocking findings\ncodex-r29.md:13:**H3 workload needs to be settled before sending.** Applying the current filter to real old-2 answers selected **244 of 247** reader judgements, about **1,464 rows** across six similar sequences. The dry count of 1,521 is therefore a realistic workload estimate. Time a representative batch of source traces, account for adjudication, and pin how an identical source trace may be reused while retaining a finding for every required occurrence. The verdict must require that work to be complete. [H3 filter](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:123) · [Dry workload](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-BUILD.md:70)\ncodex-r29.md:15:**$7 remains a reasonable ceiling to propose** after these fixes, against the **$5.89** planning estimate. It leaves room for the projected reservations and some variation in spend. A ceiling stop remains inconclusive. [Price estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/price.log:70)\ncodex-r28.md:1:**NOT READY.** The dry runs support the six-arm setup and the expected call counts. The paid entry point can still report success after a design check fails, the worksheet omits the harm evidence, and `verdict.py` can pass incomplete findings. [Dry evidence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-BUILD.md:7) · [Paid exit condition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:325)\ncodex-r28.md:3:### Blocking findings\ncodex-r28.md:10:### Price and ceiling\ncodex-r27.md:1:**READY TO BUILD THE PAID HARNESS.** Both round-26 findings are fixed. I found no remaining ambiguity in the revised credit and coverage rules that would let the same adjudicated answers produce opposite verdicts.\ncodex-r22.md:1:## Part A — READY\ncodex-r22.md:5:## Part B — NOT READY\ncodex-r22.md:17:**Allow-list.** I found no legitimate non-reader judgement change on this fixed old-2 trajectory. New links run between numeric assets, filing excludes `reads` rows, and judgement propagation queues the recorded dependants. The theme broadcast applies to changed themes, and the four fresh readers are assets. Keep the hard stop on an unexplained non-reader miss. For H2, compare the request and substantive judgement fields by question and day, since extra reader judgements can shift row IDs and the configuration checksum differs by arm. [Fixed-trajectory boundary](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:18) · [Filing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:256) · [Judgement propagation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:287)\ncodex-r22.md:19:**Cost.** Repricing the **247** old-2 reader judgements from archived usage gives **$0.872 per sequence**, close to the design’s $0.86. On the fixed schedule, I counted **19 extra judgement days per linked sequence** even if every fresh reader marks its conclusion changed. That is about **8%** more accepted judgements. The $6.8 figure is a planning estimate. Replace the 50% propagation assumption with the dry-run count and price prompt growth, retries, and live reservations separately before asking Alex for the ceiling. [Cost estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:81) · [Settlement formula](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:438) · [Reservation formula](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py:227)\ncodex-r23.md:1:**NOT READY.** The paired arms and date cutoffs are sound. The reading rule can still pass or fail because of how forecasts are parsed, which dates enter the denominator, or where a sourced figure appears in the request. Those decisions need to be fixed before building the harness.\ncodex-r23.md:3:### Arms and dry proof\ncodex-r23.md:9:### Blocking changes to the reading rule\ncodex-r23.md:16:### Cost and time\ncodex-r24.md:1:**NOT READY.** Version 3 fixes the latest-upstream proof and makes the parser a triage tool. Three gaps remain in §4. Uncertainty can become a failure, a linked double refusal can become inconclusive, and a forecast outside the H5 sample can still supply the apparent improvement.\ncodex-r24.md:3:### Blocking changes\ncodex-r24.md:11:### Thresholds and reading load\ncodex-r25.md:1:**NOT READY.** Version 4 fixes the handling of unresolved dates and linked double refusals. The remaining blocker is how P gives or withdraws credit for a gain. Two readers can apply the current wording to the same forecasts and reach opposite verdicts. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\ncodex-r25.md:3:### Blocking changes\ncodex-r25.md:8:### Round-24 findings that hold as fixed\ncodex-r26.md:1:**NOT READY.** Version 5 fixes both round-25 counterexamples. One ambiguity remains in how the credit rule handles multiple unquantifiable CPU gains. Two readers can still reach **pass** and **inconclusive** from the same answers. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89)\ncodex-r26.md:3:### Round-25 findings\ncodex-r26.md:9:### Exact remaining change\n3:Files: `paid_run.py` (plan, dry, dry-refused-upstream, send; one sequence per process), `paid_mutants.py` (five mutants of the\n7:## $0 evidence\n9:  (07-15), v21 (07-26) in every draw; the controls retire 247 rows (v14 71, v9 70, v16 56, v21 50), each reader's archive judge rows from\n10:  its first retired call on checked equal to its accepted judgements from its link day (no refused first attempt among readers).\n14:  run on every linked reader request (267 in linked-1) with no problem; the fixed trajectory (H2) holds (non-reader judgements and every\n18:- `paid-mutant-{1..5}.log`, each caught: the control not retired (freshness fails); a stale upstream recorded by the harness only\n29:## The send (after this review and Alex's ceiling)\n30:One ledger at the ceiling Alex names (`budget_replay.py --init-ledger`), then six processes at once:\n31:`paid_run.py send <out> <kind> <draw> <ledger> <ceiling>` for linked-1..3 and control-1..3. Each refuses a ceiling not equal to the\n32:ledger's, registers its launcher (the stop gate stops all when one dies), holds July's latch, and writes `summary.json`. Then the\n37:## Round 28 findings and the rebuild (`codex-r28.md`, NOT READY: four)\n38:1. **One acceptance gate** (`paid_run.acceptance`) for the dry runs and the send: the window completed with terminal `complete`; the\n51:   terminal \"double refusal\", gate refused), `paid-refused-reservation.log` (the paid entry point on a $0.000001 ledger: the first reader\n53:   `preflight-window.log` (old-2's archive and ledger) and `v120-replay-{1,2,3}.log` (the probe archives' link answers).\n58:   missing or non-boolean flag, a missing or invalid gain review for a provisionally improved pair, and a run-decided flag that disagrees\n62:## Rerun evidence ($0)\n65:- `paid-mutant-{1..6}.log`, each refused by the gate on its named check: control not retired (freshness), stale upstream recorded and\n70:## Reading load, measured on the dry worksheet\n71:P 432 rows; H4 213; H5 84; H3 1,521 in the dry run, an upper bound (every marker answer changes its reference); a real run's H3 count\n72:is known only after the run. Gain rows 18 in the synthetic reading.\n74:## Round 29 findings and the changes (`codex-r29.md`, NOT READY: three, and the H3 load)\n75:1. **Acceptance carried to the verdict:** the gate adds a clean final ledger (not stopped, no overrun); `verdict.py` requires all six\n92:their first run (the H1 days not matched, then packet files overwritten by an earlier case) and were corrected; the code under test did\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'mechanism draws findings|Small first|At least three|Write the decision|three samples|conjunction' ../../../AGENTS.md; rg -n 'pair|H1|H2|H4|Credit|Readable|Fail|Pass|Inconclusive|Price' PAID-DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"85:- When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule.\n289:- **Measure the stick before using it:** control against a second control, the criterion's null estimated (a conjunction over many items\n337:  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n354:- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n4:READY: the reading rule and the freshness proof), version 3 in round 24 (`codex-r24.md`, NOT READY: three outcomes per pair, product\n14:replaces poc-v118 (D699). It is paired on old-2's trajectory (DESIGN.md §1): every request byte-equal to one old-2 sent is answered with\n22:## 1. Arms (paired by draw n = 1, 2, 3)\n68:5. Price from old-2's archived usage of the readers' days (round 22: 247 judgements, $0.872 a sequence) plus the dry runs' added days,\n74:judgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n77:**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n78:distinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n81:class. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n83:**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n90:- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved **in the hand-read classes** (before any credit adjustment): a\n93:- **Credit (rounds 24–25; one rule):** a **gain** is a date on which the linked arm is not definite and the control is definite. Only\n94:  gains are ever adjusted; no other date's class changes for credit. When a pair is provisionally improved, **every** gain is reviewed:\n96:  from stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported) and for H4. A gain whose forecasts carry a material\n99:  the gain counts unresolved: **every** such gain of the pair is marked unresolved, and the bounds are then recomputed once across the\n100:  pair (round 26: two gains marked one at a time could each look harmless). Each date keeps its actual class in the report beside its\n101:  credit-adjusted class; P is recomputed on the credit-adjusted classes. A confirmed CPU double count stays a harm (H4) whatever its\n105:**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n106:- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n113:- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n114:  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n125:  request (the trace to the dated outside statement), and the paired arm's conclusion in force that day. On old-2's reader answers the\n127:  named only to say none is supplied). Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error whose paired control's conclusion in force on\n129:- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n133:  source-backed read whose class decides. H5 is a separate paired harm count: a linked sequence with at least 2 more material failures\n137:or harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n138:H4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n139:pairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n143:arm name, P rows only) for the blind reader, and the H packet (H3, H4, H5 rows, which reveal the arm through their requests); the gain\n145:the stores) carries a valid final finding, derives H1 from the dated judge rows of the six summaries, H3–H5 from the read rows, and each\n147:refusal fails before any packet is required. One P row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n148:both judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n149:the FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n153:first; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n154:outcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n155:about 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 rows, each read twice: 876 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews (twice where\n156:an H4 finding is made), 168 H5 marks (84 question-dates twice), the H3 traces, and the full gain review (both supplying answers of every\n169:(about 5%) and the live reservations (one judge call per sequence at once). Priced exactly from the dry runs before the ceiling\n180:   §3 items 2–3. 2. P on annual ranges, carry-forward, coverage, all three pairs, outcomes for every case, the parser validated on\n181:   flagged and unflagged stored answers: §4, `parser-validation.md`. 3. H4 on every post-link v14 answer; H3 traced outside the\n185:cases: §2, §3 item 2. Every distinct pair hand-classified, misparses recorded, carried-forward dates: §4. Paired rates on jointly\n187:link to the outside statement: §4 H3. H1 second refusal, H2 invalidates, H4 and H5 consequences in P, disagreement resolution,\n189:Round 24: three outcomes per pair from two-sided bounds over all 73 dates, the floor on known control dates, 60 stated as a policy: §4 P.\n190:Product against infrastructure failure, matched reader-days, the H1 tolerance's rationale: §4 H1 and precedence. Credit only for gains\n191:that pass rule B, the CPU increment shown removed or the pair undecided, H3 triggers on amount, scope or attribution against the\n192:control's conclusion in force: §4. The worksheet, its worked example, adjudication for P, H3, H4 and H5, reading time: §4.\n193:Round 25: every gain of a provisionally improved pair reviewed, both supplying answers, deduplicated by judgement id; only gains\n194:adjusted, by one credit rule; H4 and H5 reach P only through it; H1's boundary stated (harm at 4); reading load counted per reader:\n195:§4. The harness review will run two $0 verdict checks: a pair whose control has 2 definite dates and the linked arm none (both gains\n197:pair with both gains resting on unquantifiable CPU increments (both marked unresolved together: D_L + U_L = 2, not below D_C = 2:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '10,70p;73,172p'; nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '79,88p;326,369p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '10,70p;73,172p'; nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '79,88p;326,369p'\"","aggregated_output":"    10\t## 0. What it decides, and what it can reach\n    11\t\n    12\tIt decides whether linking (poc-v120: Jev's link questions version 2; judging-v8's fuller dependency block) makes the linked number\n    13\tquestions' judgements agree over the release window without harm elsewhere: the evidence for Alex's choice whether the linking version\n    14\treplaces poc-v118 (D699). It is paired on old-2's trajectory (DESIGN.md §1): every request byte-equal to one old-2 sent is answered with\n    15\told-2's answer; only the readers' judgements are bought.\n    16\t\n    17\t**The attainable verdict ($0):** Data Center FY2027 above total revenue FY2027 (`contradictions.py`, calendar dates 07-01..09-20,\n    18\tparser second version, before hand reading): old-1 11 days of 77, old-2 56 of 73, old-3 1 of 33 (that one a misparse,\n    19\t`parser-validation.md`). The class occurs and varies widely between draws; old-2's stored path is an extreme and not a fair control.\n    20\tA linked sequence that reconciles would show near zero; only fresh controls can serve as the baseline.\n    21\t\n    22\t## 1. Arms (paired by draw n = 1, 2, 3)\n    23\t\n    24\t- **linked-n:** poc-v120 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive. Its link jobs are answered only by probe draw n's\n    25\t  answers, matched by request hash (`window_test.probe_answers`; any other link job stops the sequence before a purchase), so its links\n    26\t  are probe draw n's proposals, now written. Readers in all three draws: v9 (Data Center FY2027, reads v14), v14 (total revenue FY2027,\n    27\t  reads v8), v16 (total CPU FY2027, reads v14), v21 (revenue growth FY2027, reads v16, and v9 in draws 2 and 3).\n    28\t- **control-n:** poc-v118 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive, its readers' old judgements retired as below.\n    29\t- **Fresh judgements, delimited by date (round 22):** a reader's link is written by an article of day D_R (v9, v14: 07-10, seq 148;\n    30\t  v16: seq 265's day; v21: seq 534's day); its first affected judgement is its first judgement on a day at or after D_R (old-2: seq 167,\n    31\t  274, 542). Every judgement of a reader on a day at or after D_R must be a fresh answer in both arms: in linked-n because its request\n    32\t  carries the dependency block, in control-n because the old request family is retired. The old family of a reader is identified by\n    33\t  reader and judgement day: every archive judge row of that reader (accepted answers, first attempts the stored run refused, and their\n    34\t  retries) whose judgement day is at or after D_R; archive rows carry no day, so a row is assigned to a day through old-2's\n    35\t  `judgements` table (accepted rows) and through its request text (the refused first attempts, whose request equals the retry's before\n    36\t  the correction lines). Every retired row is listed before the send and checked against old-2's judgements by reader and day.\n    37\t- **old-2's stored path:** a labelled historical baseline, outside the fresh-control figures.\n    38\t- All six sequences run at once, one paid call at a time within each (July's latch, round 22), one shared reservation ledger at the\n    39\t  ceiling Alex names.\n    40\t\n    41\t## 2. Harness rules (July's, `dependency-design/july_test.py`, extended to the window)\n    42\t\n    43\t- Harness-only marker, the store lock, never resumed; a stop is final, and its verdict follows §4's precedence (a linked double refusal\n    44\t  fails; an infrastructure stop or a control's double refusal makes the comparison inconclusive).\n    45\t- **Allow-list before the budget transport, per sequence, under July's latch** (admission and the inner call under one lock; the first\n    46\t  refusal closes the sequence): a linked sequence may buy only judge requests of its readers on days at or after their D_R; a control\n    47\t  only the retired readers' judge requests on those days; a link job only from the probe draw. Admission is bound to the replay's\n    48\t  judging day (the harness wraps `round.judge_day` to know the day of every judge request), not to the request's text. Any other request that misses the archive\n    49\t  stops the sequence (round 22 found no legitimate non-reader change on this trajectory). The four answers old-2's own run refused are\n    50\t  given back once each for non-readers, as in the `neither` pass.\n    51\t- Settlement at published rates with cache writes at 1.25x; reservation from request size (D696).\n    52\t\n    53\t## 3. The $0 proofs before the send\n    54\t\n    55\t1. `v120-replay` (done): poc-v120 with each probe draw's own answers meets all 17 link jobs and ends equal to old-2.\n    56\t2. **Linked dry run per draw** (a fake judge answering readers with markers): committed links equal probe draw n's proposals; no link\n    57\t   job outside the probe; no circle correction; every non-reader judgement equal to old-2's by question and day in its request and its\n    58\t   substantive fields (rung, reasons, reference, implied outcome, cites); **freshness checked on the archive side as well as the\n    59\t   transport side** (round 23: a cache hit bypasses the transport): every reader judgement made on a day at or after its D_R has a call\n    60\t   id above the copied archive's top, and none before; **the upstream snapshot:** when each reader prompt is built, the harness records\n    61\t   the judgement id, date, reference and forecast of each question it reads (wrapping `store.current_state` as `judging.build_prompt`\n    62\t   calls it) and checks the rendered dependency block against that snapshot, never against the final ledger; the cases a same-day\n    63\t   upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer (old-2's v14 has ten judgement days with no v8 judgement that day) and a refused\n    64\t   upstream answer followed by its accepted retry are each exercised; the added days listed.\n    65\t3. **Control dry run per draw:** the retired rows listed and checked; only the retired readers reach the transport, on the right days;\n    66\t   every other judgement equal to old-2's by question and day.\n    67\t4. The stop paths and the latch race, on the window.\n    68\t5. Price from old-2's archived usage of the readers' days (round 22: 247 judgements, $0.872 a sequence) plus the dry runs' added days,\n    69\t   prompt growth from the dependency block, retries, and the live reservations, each shown apart.\n    70\t\n    73\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    74\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    75\tforce that day.\n    76\t\n    77\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    78\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    79\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    80\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    81\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n    84\t(overlap or clear, N) or unresolved (U), each date keeping its own arm's class even where the other arm is unresolved. Linked-n's\n    85\tdefinite count lies between D_L and D_L + U_L; control-n's between D_C and D_C + U_C.\n    86\t- **Improved:** D_L + U_L < D_C (the least favourable completion still has fewer definite dates in the linked arm).\n    87\t- **Not improved:** D_L >= D_C + U_C (even the most favourable completion cannot give the linked arm fewer).\n    88\t- **Undecided:** otherwise (the unresolved dates could change the result).\n    89\t- **Testable:** D_C >= 2 (known definite dates of the control over all 73, not only dates readable in both arms).\n    90\t- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved **in the hand-read classes** (before any credit adjustment): a\n    91\t  coverage policy that deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable dates per arm (82.2%). A readable forecast with unsupported arithmetic\n    92\t  stays readable; its uncertainty reaches P through the credit-adjusted classes the bounds use.\n    93\t- **Credit (rounds 24–25; one rule):** a **gain** is a date on which the linked arm is not definite and the control is definite. Only\n    94\t  gains are ever adjusted; no other date's class changes for credit. When a pair is provisionally improved, **every** gain is reviewed:\n    95\t  both linked answers in force on it (v9's and v14's), deduplicated by judgement id, are read for July's rule B (arithmetic following\n    96\t  from stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported) and for H4. A gain whose forecasts carry a material\n    97\t  rule-B failure on which its reconciliation rests earns no credit (counted definite for P). A gain resting on an unsupported CPU\n    98\t  increment: when the increment can be quantified, the forecast with it removed gives the gain's credit-adjusted class; when it cannot,\n    99\t  the gain counts unresolved: **every** such gain of the pair is marked unresolved, and the bounds are then recomputed once across the\n   100\t  pair (round 26: two gains marked one at a time could each look harmless). Each date keeps its actual class in the report beside its\n   101\t  credit-adjusted class; P is recomputed on the credit-adjusted classes. A confirmed CPU double count stays a harm (H4) whatever its\n   102\t  date.\n   103\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n   106\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n   107\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n   108\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm at **4 or more** refusals above its control on matched reader-days (3 extra is tolerated).\n   109\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   110\t  4 extra is far above that base rate while up to 3 stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   111\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   112\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   113\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   114\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   115\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   116\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   117\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   118\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   119\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. **Selection (round 29: the earlier trigger selected 244 of 247 reader answers on\n   120\t  old-2, since the reference field always speaks of the market):** a structural rule from the exact request, the same in both arms\n   121\t  (`worksheet.structural_flag`): a reference amount found among the system's own figures in the request (a read judgement's comparison\n   122\t  point or forecast, or the previous conclusion) and nowhere in the evidence (\"own figure as market\"); or a part question (Data Center,\n   123\t  CPU) whose reference names a consensus with an amount found in the evidence (\"scope check\"); plus every reader answer in force on the\n   124\t  seven fixed dates below. Every selected row is read with its request, the read judgements it was shown and each read judgement's own\n   125\t  request (the trace to the dated outside statement), and the paired arm's conclusion in force that day. On old-2's reader answers the\n   126\t  rule selects 90 of 247 (80 own-figure, 10 scope), many of them cleared on reading (a guide amount written another way, a consensus\n   127\t  named only to say none is supplied). Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error whose paired control's conclusion in force on\n   128\t  that date does not have the same error type.\n   129\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   130\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above.\n   131\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   132\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   133\t  source-backed read whose class decides. H5 is a separate paired harm count: a linked sequence with at least 2 more material failures\n   134\t  than its control is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above (a shared error moves no date).\n   135\t\n   136\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   137\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   138\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   139\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   140\t\n   141\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; rounds 24, 28, 29):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run,\n   142\tits format and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send, in **two packets**: the P packet (blind: shuffled labels, no\n   143\tarm name, P rows only) for the blind reader, and the H packet (H3, H4, H5 rows, which reveal the arm through their requests); the gain\n   144\tpacket follows the P reading. `verdict.py` takes the read packets themselves: it refuses unless every expected H row (regenerated from\n   145\tthe stores) carries a valid final finding, derives H1 from the dated judge rows of the six summaries, H3–H5 from the read rows, and each\n   146\tgain's credit from the reviews of its two supplying answers; it requires all six summaries accepted by the gate, and a linked double\n   147\trefusal fails before any packet is required. One P row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n   148\tboth judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n   149\tthe FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n   150\tthe exact request text (dependency block marked), the read judgement each figure came through, and the dated source quotes. Worked\n   151\texample (old-1 09-08): Data Center \"$419 billion for FY2027\" against total revenue \"$414.3 billion ... supports roughly $414–421\n   152\tbillion\": low end 419 is not above high end 421, so **overlap**, though the parser took $414.3B and flagged it definite. Claude reads\n   153\tfirst; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n   154\toutcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n   155\tabout 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 rows, each read twice: 876 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews (twice where\n   156\tan H4 finding is made), 168 H5 marks (84 question-dates twice), the H3 traces, and the full gain review (both supplying answers of every\n   157\tgain, deduplicated), before adjudication; the dry review counts each reader's rows apart; estimated 8–12 hours of reading across the\n   158\treaders, apart from the run's three hours.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Reported beside, not gating:** each link's relation, direction and sign (the weak CPU link disclosed); self-made references\n   161\t(`reference_census.py`'s measures, the `unsourced` scan with the dependency block excluded); v14 linked against control; v21's implied\n   162\ttotal against v9 and v14; linked-only judgement days.\n   163\t\n   164\t## 5. Cost (estimate; the ceiling is Alex's)\n   165\t\n   166\tReader judgements from their links, at published rates from old-2's archived usage: $0.872 a sequence (247 judgements, round 22).\n   167\tLinked sequences: about 19 added judgement days each (round 22's count on the fixed schedule; the dry runs give the exact count) and\n   168\tthe dependency block's prompt growth (about 10%): about $1.05. Three linked and three controls: about $5.8, plus retries and refusals\n   169\t(about 5%) and the live reservations (one judge call per sequence at once). Priced exactly from the dry runs before the ceiling\n   170\tquestion.\n   171\t\n   172\t## 6. Time\n    79\treject\", a cent a case).\n    80\t\n    81\tThen converge or stop:\n    82\t- A mechanism named on its counted class is fixed in the same slice and measured, and the report leads with it. A root-caused defect is\n    83\t  never parked \"for Alex\"; a parked fix is an open defect. Go to Alex only when the mechanism is not found, or the fix would change the\n    84\t  measuring stick or the PRD.\n    85\t- When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule.\n    86\t- A failure called a flake is a defect until traced. Read a failing check to its first differing line before any rerun; a passing rerun\n    87\t  proves nothing.\n    88\t- A subagent's or reviewer's report is a lead. Read the code, contract or record it rests on before acting on it or recording it, and\n   326\t### 7.3 Iterating\n   327\t\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   346\t- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n   347\t  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n   348\t  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   349\t  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n   350\t  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n   351\t  the next test goes straight ahead, held to the PRD and its quantified goals, with every trace read by you yourself and not only\n   352\t  by readers (Alex, 2026-11-10: \"你应该跑council loop 来reflect 你每一轮的思路还有决策，需要你打成共识，记录决策，向前直行测试。不要drift,我们有PRD和量化目标，\n   353\t  你自己作为llm 下去看所有trace\").\n   354\t- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n   355\t  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n   356\t  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n   357\t  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n   358\t  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n   359\t  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n   360\t  control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the model's own account and the\n   361\t  request locate where the decision is made (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你跑这么多次真的有意义吗\"; \"我感觉你在为了跑而跑\"). Several fixes on one\n   362\t  base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02: \"快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整\"; D266).\n   363\t- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n   364\t  rounds of review (Claude's own, Codex's, an independent reader's) on the traces until a round finds nothing serious; never a menu of\n   365\t  arms or options, never a conclusion the next probe overturns (a choice that changes what the system does still goes to him with its\n   366\t  options and a recommendation, Authority and scope) (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"就测试一种，一次 … 你就没有自己看5轮review，能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议？\").\n   367\t  An agreed recommendation is first challenged by an independent reader on the concrete evidence behind it (\"why you give\n   368\t  recommendations for this. What did you see exactly?\"). A review round reads a rule as its author meant it; only a send shows how the\n   369\t  model applies it: before a registered test, the changed request is sent on the cases that motivated it, a few cents.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in codex-r{22,23,24,25,26,27,28,29,30}.md; do wc -l \"$f\"; done; nl -ba PAID-BUILD.md | sed -n '\"'1,110p'; rg -n '\"'^## D(699|705|706|707)|Small first'\"' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in codex-r{22,23,24,25,26,27,28,29,30}.md; do wc -l \"$f\"; done; nl -ba PAID-BUILD.md | sed -n '\"'1,110p'; rg -n '\"'^## D(699|705|706|707)|Small first'\"' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"      18 codex-r22.md\n      17 codex-r23.md\n      15 codex-r24.md\n      12 codex-r25.md\n      12 codex-r26.md\n       5 codex-r27.md\n      11 codex-r28.md\n      14 codex-r29.md\n      15 codex-r30.md\n     1\t# The paid harness, built (PAID-DESIGN.md version 6, D707; for council review before any send)\n     2\t\n     3\tFiles: `paid_run.py` (plan, dry, dry-refused-upstream, send; one sequence per process), `paid_mutants.py` (five mutants of the\n     4\tharness's own checks), `worksheet.py` (the pinned reading worksheet), `verdict.py` (§4's verdict from the read worksheet and the harm\n     5\tfindings, with its checks), `contradictions.py` (the lead parser).\n     6\t\n     7\t## $0 evidence\n     8\t- `plan.json`: the three draws' links (from the probe draws, candidate ids resolved to v14, v16, v21), readers v9, v14 (07-10), v16\n     9\t  (07-15), v21 (07-26) in every draw; the controls retire 247 rows (v14 71, v9 70, v16 56, v21 50), each reader's archive judge rows from\n    10\t  its first retired call on checked equal to its accepted judgements from its link day (no refused first attempt among readers).\n    11\t- `paid-dry-{linked,control}-{1,2,3}.log` (a marker judge for every bought judgement, every answer \"changed\", so propagation is at its\n    12\t  largest): all six complete; links committed exactly the probe draw's (none in controls); every probe link job met; every post-link\n    13\t  reader judgement fresh (call id above the copied archive's top) and every pre-link one from the archive; the upstream snapshot check\n    14\t  run on every linked reader request (267 in linked-1) with no problem; the fixed trajectory (H2) holds (non-reader judgements and every\n    15\t  other table equal to old-2's). Bought: 266 per linked sequence (19 days added by propagation), 247 per control.\n    16\t- `paid-dry-refused-upstream.log`: v14's first attempt on its third judgement refused (an evidence id it was not shown), its retry\n    17\t  accepted; no request shows the refused answer; 267 bought (the retry); snapshot checks clean.\n    18\t- `paid-mutant-{1..5}.log`, each caught: the control not retired (freshness fails); a stale upstream recorded by the harness only\n    19\t  (snapshot problems); v21 missing from the allow-list (the sequence stops on 07-26); a probe link answer missing (stops on 08-06 at the\n    20\t  link job); a non-reader admitted and bought (H2 fails).\n    21\t- `verdict-checks.log`: 14 checks, every one as specified, including round 25's (both gains withdrawn: not improved; one withdrawn alone:\n    22\t  improved; a shared error on a clear date cannot be adjusted) and round 26's (two unquantifiable CPU gains: undecided), round 24's\n    23\t  (5 control definite, 13 linked unresolved: undecided) and the precedence.\n    24\t- `worksheet-dry.log`: the worksheet on the dry stores: 72 distinct pairs per sequence covering the 73 dates (432 rows).\n    25\t- `price.log`: from the dry counts and old-2's archived usage at published rates (cache writes 1.25x): linked about $1.06 each (+10%\n    26\t  prompt growth, +5% retries), controls about $0.91 each; about $5.89 in all; the largest single reservation $0.043, six live at once\n    27\t  about $0.26.\n    28\t\n    29\t## The send (after this review and Alex's ceiling)\n    30\tOne ledger at the ceiling Alex names (`budget_replay.py --init-ledger`), then six processes at once:\n    31\t`paid_run.py send <out> <kind> <draw> <ledger> <ceiling>` for linked-1..3 and control-1..3. Each refuses a ceiling not equal to the\n    32\tledger's, registers its launcher (the stop gate stops all when one dies), holds July's latch, and writes `summary.json`. Then the\n    33\tworksheet, the hand reading (Claude, a blind reader, third reads), the findings file and `verdict.py`.\n    34\t\n    35\tRecommended ceiling to ask Alex: $7 (the estimate plus the live reservations and about 15% margin).\n    36\t\n    37\t## Round 28 findings and the rebuild (`codex-r28.md`, NOT READY: four)\n    38\t1. **One acceptance gate** (`paid_run.acceptance`) for the dry runs and the send: the window completed with terminal `complete`; the\n    39\t   committed graph equal to `pinned-plan.json`'s, labels included (none in a control); the probe jobs consumed exactly the pinned set;\n    40\t   freshness; the snapshot; H2. `send` exits non-zero when it fails. The judging day is bound inside `round.judge_day` and cleared when\n    41\t   it returns; a request is admitted only when its schema is `judgement` (unit check: a request titled \"Thesis v14\" without it, sent\n    42\t   while a day is set, is not admitted).\n    43\t2. **Decisive checks:** freshness is now \"the accepted judgement's call was made during its own day's judging\" (the archive's top before\n    44\t   and after each `judge_day`; unit check: a call in another day's window fails); the snapshot compares each dependency entry, found by\n    45\t   its own target id, line for line with that target's recorded judgement day, comparison point, forecast and reasons excerpt, and the\n    46\t   snapshots are written to `snapshots.jsonl`; H2 uses the pinned reader set, never the admission table, and compares typed keys and every\n    47\t   substantive judgement field (pushes, theme pushes, why changed).\n    48\t3. **Stop paths, typed:** each sequence records every judge attempt at the transport (reader, day, request hash, retry, admitted) and\n    49\t   every judge row written with its day, and a terminal outcome (`complete`, `double refusal` with the reader and day, or `infrastructure\n    50\t   stop` with its reason). Exercised: `paid-double-refusal-linked.log` and `-control.log` (v9 refused twice on its second judgement:\n    51\t   terminal \"double refusal\", gate refused), `paid-refused-reservation.log` (the paid entry point on a $0.000001 ledger: the first reader\n    52\t   reservation refused, terminal \"infrastructure stop\", exit 1, nothing sent), the latch race (`paid-units.log`). Preflight on the inputs:\n    53\t   `preflight-window.log` (old-2's archive and ledger) and `v120-replay-{1,2,3}.log` (the probe archives' link answers).\n    54\t4. **The worksheet and the verdict:** `worksheet.py build` writes P rows under shuffled labels (key outside the repository) and H rows\n    55\t   with the exact requests, the read judgement ids shown and the paired control's conclusion in force (H4), the seven fixed dates (H5),\n    56\t   and reference changes or market-figure keywords (H3); `worksheet.py gains` writes the gain review rows after P is read (both linked\n    57\t   answers per gain, deduplicated). `verdict.py` refuses: off-window dates, a date read twice or missing, a class outside the four, a\n    58\t   missing or non-boolean flag, a missing or invalid gain review for a provisionally improved pair, and a run-decided flag that disagrees\n    59\t   with the six summaries (`worksheet-verdict-dry.log`: a synthetic reading of the dry stores gives \"pass\"; each of six bad inputs is\n    60\t   refused).\n    61\t\n    62\t## Rerun evidence ($0)\n    63\t- `paid-dry-{linked,control}-{1,2,3}.log`: all six accepted by the gate; 266 / 247 bought; 266 snapshot checks per linked sequence.\n    64\t- `paid-dry-refused-upstream.log`: accepted, 267 bought, the refused answer shown to no request.\n    65\t- `paid-mutant-{1..6}.log`, each refused by the gate on its named check: control not retired (freshness), stale upstream recorded and\n    66\t  swapped upstreams (snapshot), a reader missing from the allow-list and a probe answer missing (window not completed), a non-reader\n    67\t  bought (H2, now in the summary itself).\n    68\t- `verdict-checks.log` (14), `paid-units.log` (3), `worksheet-verdict-dry.log`.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Reading load, measured on the dry worksheet\n    71\tP 432 rows; H4 213; H5 84; H3 1,521 in the dry run, an upper bound (every marker answer changes its reference); a real run's H3 count\n    72\tis known only after the run. Gain rows 18 in the synthetic reading.\n    73\t\n    74\t## Round 29 findings and the changes (`codex-r29.md`, NOT READY: three, and the H3 load)\n    75\t1. **Acceptance carried to the verdict:** the gate adds a clean final ledger (not stopped, no overrun); `verdict.py` requires all six\n    76\t   summaries accepted and evaluates terminal outcomes first (a linked double refusal fails with no packet required; an infrastructure\n    77\t   stop, a control double refusal, an H2 difference or a rejected summary is inconclusive).\n    78\t2. **Reviews required, conclusions derived:** the verdict regenerates the expected H rows from the stores and refuses any missing,\n    79\t   unexpected or unread row; H1 from the summaries' dated judge rows on matched reader-days; H3 (a linked grounding error unmatched by\n    80\t   the paired control's conclusion in force), H4 (a double count), H5 (2 or more extra material failures) from the read rows; each gain's\n    81\t   credit from its two supplying answers' reviews (`gain_credit`).\n    82\t3. **Packets:** the P packet alone is blind (no arm name, no H rows); the H packet carries, per row, the exact request, the read\n    83\t   judgements shown with their own requests (the trace), and the paired arm's conclusion in force; H3 now includes every reader's\n    84\t   conclusion in force on the seven fixed dates (v21 on 07-26 among them).\n    85\t4. **H3 load:** the selection is now the structural rule (PAID-DESIGN.md §4 H3): 90 of 247 on old-2's reader answers, about 700 rows\n    86\t   across six sequences with the fixed dates, against about 1,464 under the earlier trigger.\n    87\t\n    88\t`e2e-checks.log` ($0, the dry stores, packets built and a synthetic reading): a complete reading passes; refused or failing as\n    89\tspecified: P rows only, an unread H row, an incomplete gain review, every gain withdrawn (not improved: fail), an H4 double count, a\n    90\tlinked grounding error the control lacks, two more H5 failures, a rejected summary (inconclusive), a linked double refusal with no P\n    91\trows (fail), four extra refusals on matched reader-days (H1 fail) and three (tolerated: pass). Two of my own check fixtures were wrong on\n    92\ttheir first run (the H1 days not matched, then packet files overwritten by an earlier case) and were corrected; the code under test did\n    93\tnot change. `h3-load.log`: the structural rule on old-2's 247 reader answers.\n4412:- **Scope:** a probe of a few cents (AGENTS §7.3, \"Small first, then one run\"). It measures nothing: six answers, one draw.\n10505:## D699 · 2026-11-21 · Alex continues with A: the whole-window measurement of the linking version, designed with the council at $0 first\n10583:## D705 · 2026-11-21 · The version-2 link question passes its registered gate; council R: version 2 goes to the paid design\n10594:## D706 · 2026-11-21 · poc-v120 (link questions version 2) passes council review as a product configuration; the paid design needs four changes\n10611:## D707 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the paid design (version 6, rounds 22–27) for building its harness\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 28 29 30; do nl -ba \"codex-r$n.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 28 29 30; do nl -ba \"codex-r$n.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**NOT READY.** The dry runs support the six-arm setup and the expected call counts. The paid entry point can still report success after a design check fails, the worksheet omits the harm evidence, and `verdict.py` can pass incomplete findings. [Dry evidence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-BUILD.md:7) · [Paid exit condition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:325)\n     2\t\n     3\t### Blocking findings\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Use one acceptance gate for dry and paid runs.** The four readers’ cutoff dates and 247 retirements per control check out. But `send` returns success whenever `stopped` is empty, even after a freshness, snapshot, graph, or H2 failure. The dry graph check compares endpoints and sequence numbers while dropping the direction label, and it checks the number of probe jobs rather than the exact jobs consumed. Gate both paths on a completed window, the exact committed graph including labels, exact probe-job consumption, and the freshness and H2 checks. Clear the judging-day scope when `judge_day` exits and verify the request is a judgement before admission. I reproduced a request with only a `Thesis v14` title reaching the inner transport while the day was set. [Retirement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:84) · [Admission and day scope](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:127) · [Dry checks and paid exit](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:303)\n     6\t2. **Make freshness, upstream provenance, and H2 decisive.** A call ID above the copied archive’s top proves the answer was created sometime in this sequence. It can still be a cache hit on a later day. Tie each accepted judgement to its own dated fresh attempt. The snapshot check searches the whole prompt for the expected values. I swapped the two upstream conclusions in a `v21` fixture and the check passed. Compare each dependency’s rendered block against its own target, judgement date, reference, and forecast, and persist the snapshot for reading. The H2 mutant bought **62** `v8` judgements while its normal summary still said H2 passed. The test caught it only by rerunning a different comparison with a hard-coded reader set. Use the pinned plan independently of admission for H2, and include the omitted typed keys and substantive judgement fields. [Snapshot and freshness checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:154) · [Archive-hit path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:251) · [H2 comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:210) · [Mutant override](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_mutants.py:50)\n     7\t3. **Record and exercise the stop paths.** The missing-reader and missing-probe mutants show their intended refusals. The refused-upstream dry run shows an accepted retry. The supplied evidence does not exercise the paid-path latch race, a refused reservation, or terminal double refusals in both arms. The summary stores a stop string and a list of refused view IDs. It cannot produce H1’s paired reader-day counts or reliably distinguish a linked double refusal, which fails, from infrastructure failure, which is inconclusive. Record dated attempts and typed terminal outcomes, then test those cases through the paid entry point with a fake transport. Include the required preflight on the inputs to be sent. [Required dry proofs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:52) · [Summary fields](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:248) · [Production double-refusal status](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/round.py:83) · [Preflight rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:177)\n     8\t4. **Finish the worksheet and make the verdict refuse incomplete inputs.** The 432-row dry worksheet demonstrates date carry-forward and the primary forecast fields. It lacks the exact requests, upstream judgement IDs, dated source quotes, and H3–H5 review rows, and it exposes `linked` and `control` labels. The verdict’s arithmetic matches §4 on valid, complete inputs, and all 14 supplied checks passed. Its input handling does not enforce §4. I reproduced a **pass** with 73 off-window date labels and `{}` as the findings file, and another with an invalid credit class. Require the exact calendar, unique sequence-date rows, valid classes, all gain reviews and explicit harm findings. Connect the guard to the six run summaries and exercise the worksheet-to-verdict path on dry artifacts. [Pinned format](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:135) · [Worksheet builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:33) · [Verdict input handling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:21)\n     9\t\n    10\t### Price and ceiling\n    11\t\n    12\tI independently reproduced **$5.89** from archived usage and the dry call counts, including cache writes and the stated growth and retry assumptions. The largest reservation I found in the marker runs was about **$0.0381**, below the log’s **$0.043** planning figure. Live requests and failed transport attempts can cost more than that estimate assumes. **$7 is a reasonable ceiling to propose after the fixes**, with roughly **$0.85** above estimated spend and six planning reservations. A ceiling stop remains a possible inconclusive outcome. [Price log](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/price.log:70) · [Reservation and failed-attempt charging](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py:268)     1\t**NOT READY.** The rebuild closes several round-28 gaps, but I reproduced two false passes: one using a sequence the acceptance gate rejected, and another with no completed harm or gain-judgement reviews.\n     2\t\n     3\t### What holds as fixed\n     4\t\n     5\t`send` now uses the same acceptance gate as `dry`. The gate compares the labelled graph and exact probe hashes, and checks freshness against each judgement day’s archive window. The judging-day scope clears on exit, and a title alone can no longer admit a request. H2 uses the pinned reader set, so the non-reader purchase mutant now fails in its own summary. The stale and swapped upstream mutants are caught, and the logs show typed outcomes for double refusals in both arms and a reservation refusal with zero spend. [Admission](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:156) · [Acceptance gate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:354) · [Rerun evidence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-BUILD.md:48)\n     6\t\n     7\t### Blocking findings\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **Carry the acceptance decision through to the verdict.** I substituted the actual no-retirement mutant’s summary for `control-1`. Its gate says `accepted: false`, but the verdict still returned **pass** because it reads the terminal outcome and H2 and ignores the failed freshness check. The paid gate itself also accepts a completed summary with `ledger.stopped` and `ledger.overrun` both true. Require the exact completed window and a clean final ledger, and require all six accepted summaries for an ordinary comparison. Evaluate terminal outcomes before requiring the full P worksheet. I confirmed that a linked double refusal with missing P rows currently raises a coverage error instead of returning the prescribed **fail**. [Paid gate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:337) · [Rejected mutant](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid-mutant-1.log:6) · [Verdict handoff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:66) · [Verdict entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:135)\n    10\t2. **Require the underlying reviews, not just their conclusions.** Exact dates, duplicate rows, invalid classes, missing flags, and missing gain *dates* are now checked. I still got **pass** with P rows only, all harm booleans set to false, and `credit` entered per gain date. No H3–H5 row or review of either supplying judgement was required. Validate completed and adjudicated reviews against the worksheet’s expected judgement IDs. Derive H1 from the dated attempts and derive gain credit from the two supplying reviews. Add the P-only false pass and the rejected-summary case to the end-to-end checks. [Gain rows](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:154) · [Verdict inputs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:135)\n    11\t3. **Finish the reading packets.** P labels are shuffled, but H rows in the same file contain the label and the real arm name. I recovered all six mappings without the key. Give the blind reader a separate packet. H3 also omits the paired control conclusion in force and does not explicitly include the seven fixed dates. I found **six missing H3 entries** in the dry stores: `v21` on July 26 in each sequence. Include those fixed-date rows, and give readers a trace from each linked figure through the supplying judgement to its dated outside source. [H-row construction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:99) · [Packet assembly](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:136) · [Pinned H3 and reading rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n    12\t\n    13\t**H3 workload needs to be settled before sending.** Applying the current filter to real old-2 answers selected **244 of 247** reader judgements, about **1,464 rows** across six similar sequences. The dry count of 1,521 is therefore a realistic workload estimate. Time a representative batch of source traces, account for adjudication, and pin how an identical source trace may be reused while retaining a finding for every required occurrence. The verdict must require that work to be complete. [H3 filter](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:123) · [Dry workload](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-BUILD.md:70)\n    14\t\n    15\t**$7 remains a reasonable ceiling to propose** after these fixes, against the **$5.89** planning estimate. It leaves room for the projected reservations and some variation in spend. A ceiling stop remains inconclusive. [Price estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/price.log:70)     1\t**NOT READY.** The rebuild closes the round-29 rejected-summary and missing-packet paths. H3 still misses cases it is meant to guard, and I reproduced a pass with an incomplete gain review and a failure from duplicate H5 rows.\n     2\t\n     3\t### Round-29 fixes that hold\n     4\t\n     5\tThe paid gate now rejects a stopped or overrun ledger. The verdict requires all six summaries to be accepted for an ordinary comparison and handles a linked double refusal before requiring a worksheet. The P packet is separate and carries shuffled labels. The H packet includes the fixed-date reader answers, upstream requests, and the paired conclusion in force. The end-to-end checks demonstrate the earlier regressions, including H1’s three-versus-four refusal boundary. One earlier acceptance detail remains open: the gate infers completion from replay returning without an exception. Check the recorded completed calendar against the planned window. [Acceptance gate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/paid_run.py:354) · [Verdict entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:207) · [Worksheet packets](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:150) · [End-to-end results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/e2e-checks.log:416)\n     6\t\n     7\t### Blocking findings\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **H3 uses the same selector in both arms, but the selector misses the specified harm.** I tested a v9 answer that presents the linked **$400B company forecast** as an analyst estimate for Data Center. The selector flags it until `$400B` also appears in the ladder. `sections()` puts the ladder in the same bucket as evidence, so a rung boundary suppresses the hit. The selector also misses a market claim made only in the reasons, a company estimate presented as segment guidance without the word *consensus*, and percentages such as v21’s growth reference. Separate actual evidence from prompt scaffolding, select market claims in the reference and reasons across the units these readers use, and exercise those cases outside the fixed dates. When a linked row has an error, require a finding on its paired control conclusion even if the selector missed that control row. An absent review currently counts as absence of the error. The dry run had **zero structural hits**, so its injected H3 finding tests verdict routing, not selection. [H3 specification](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:115) · [Selector](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:99) · [Paired H3 comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:159) · [Dry selection count](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/e2e-checks.log:1)\n    10\t2. **Validate review records before counting them.** On the dry artifacts, I marked a gain’s supplying answer as a material Rule B failure and deleted `reconciliation rests on it`. The verdict still **passed**. Require an explicit finding on reliance whenever a material failure is marked. Also, the H-key set check collapses duplicate rows, but H5 counts the original list. I duplicated a single H5 failure and got **fail** for two failures. Reject duplicate review keys and bind P and gain reviews to the actual supplying judgement IDs in the stores. A quantified CPU correction also needs to be applied **per gain date**. The same corrected company forecast can be clear against one day’s segment forecast and definite against the next, while the current worksheet stores one adjusted class per company judgement. [H-row validation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:221) · [H5 counting and gain validation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/verdict.py:170) · [Gain worksheet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/worksheet.py:215)\n    11\t\n    12\t### Reading time\n    13\t\n    14\tI reproduced **90 structural hits** on old-2, rising to **106 distinct rows** once fixed dates are included. That projects **636 H3 rows** over six similar sequences before extra linked hits and paired-control follow-ups. I would budget **18–30 reviewer-hours** for the full protocol, separate from the roughly three-hour run. That assumes 20–30 seconds for each of the 864 dry-schedule P marks, 30–60 seconds per H3 trace at roughly 700 rows, and additional time for H4, both H5 reads, gain review, and adjudication. The design’s **8–12 hours** is too tight for that workload. Recount after fixing H3 and time a representative batch before sending. [H3 load](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/h3-load.log:1) · [Protocol workload](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:141)\n    15\t\n    16\t**$7 remains a reasonable ceiling to propose once the blockers are fixed.**","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 22 23 24 25 26 27; do nl -ba \"codex-r$n.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 22 23 24 25 26 27; do nl -ba \"codex-r$n.md\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t## Part A — READY\n     2\t\n     3\tThe loader now accepts `link_questions` only in `matching` under `jev-links`, and only as one of the two exact strings. Omission selects V1. I reran the five rejection cases and the three config mutants in memory, checked both old checksums, and verified that both retired probe commands exit before replay. The value table now records the parallel manifest reader and its existing refusal when the fingerprint changes. [Loader](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/config.py:89) · [Tests](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_links.py:374) · [Mutation log](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/mutants.log:21) · [Value table](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/WORDING-V2.md:78) · [Retired commands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:443)\n     4\t\n     5\t## Part B — NOT READY\n     6\t\n     7\tThe paired arms are sound. Four design changes are needed before building.\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **Use the link date to delimit fresh judgements, and verify what each request actually saw.** The links at seq 148, 265 and 534 first affect judgements at seq **167, 274 and 542**, respectively. Identify the old request families by reader and judgement date, including their first attempts and retries. Call IDs identify archive rows to retire. They do not establish when a judgement became eligible. Require a fresh answer for each eligible reader judgement after its link in both arms, including an exact match to an old request. Later control requests can diverge from old-2 because they carry their own previous answers and citations. The dry proof must check the **latest accepted upstream conclusion available when the request was built**. In old-2, `v14` has ten judgement dates on which `v8` has no same-day judgement. Keep the paired probe graph and the four stored non-reader refusals in the replay. [Arm and retirement rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:24) · [Dry proof](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:47) · [Latest-state lookup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:154) · [Evidence carried forward](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:42) · [Refusal replay](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:69)\n    10\t\n    11\t2. **Make P compare the annual forecasts and define one outcome for every case.** The first-amount parser can pick the wrong figure or discard a range endpoint. The old-2 July 12 Data Center answer says **$400–410B**. The design’s example extracts $410B and §6 says it counts the low end. Freeze the comparison on the same post-link calendar dates, with an explicit carry-forward rule. Count a definite contradiction when the segment’s low endpoint exceeds the company’s high endpoint. Report overlapping ranges and missing or ambiguous annual forecasts separately, with a minimum coverage rule. Validate both flagged and unflagged dates on stored answers. The proposed `1 in 20` assumes six exchangeable ranks. It does not follow from three paired draws, especially with ties and the control floor. I would require improvement in all three paired rates once coverage and the control floor are met. Specify that confirmed material harm fails, insufficient coverage or a budget stop is inconclusive, and a complete eligible comparison missing the improvement bar fails. [Parser and primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:10) · [Pass, fail and inconclusive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:58) · [Parser caveat](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:94)\n    12\t\n    13\t3. **Make the harm checks cover the ways the chain can appear to improve.** For H4, read **every** post-link `v14` answer against the exact CPU conclusion it received and its paired fresh control. Check its reference, forecast and arithmetic whether or not `v8` is cited. Adding the whole CPU forecast to a company estimate that already includes it is harm. A supported increment over the amount already included is a different case. Record unresolved overlap explicitly. For H3, use keyword hits to guide reading and trace claimed market figures to outside statements of the right scope. The existing `unsourced` scan searches the dependency block, so copying a system forecast can clear it. Give a material arithmetic or sourcing error found in the July-style review an effect on the verdict. Restore the per-link report of relation, direction and sign, with the known weak CPU link disclosed. Finally, separate model refusals from infrastructure stops and give H1 an explicit comparison unit and denominator. [Harm rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:68) · [Reference scan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/self-reference/reference_census.py:49) · [Per-link review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:167) · [July arithmetic rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:5)\n    14\t\n    15\t4. **Keep July’s latch for the first build.** Run all six sequences concurrently, with one paid call at a time per sequence. Moving the inner call outside the lock lets an admitted worker reach the transport, or start an internal retry, after a different worker has closed the local latch. The shared ledger checks the shared stop, while the proposed refusal closes only the local latch. If intra-sequence concurrency is needed, specify which admitted calls can still start, how retries are gated, and how outstanding calls settle before final reporting. Prove those cases with a race test. [Concurrency proposal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [July latch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:56) · [Transport retries](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py:268)\n    16\t\n    17\t**Allow-list.** I found no legitimate non-reader judgement change on this fixed old-2 trajectory. New links run between numeric assets, filing excludes `reads` rows, and judgement propagation queues the recorded dependants. The theme broadcast applies to changed themes, and the four fresh readers are assets. Keep the hard stop on an unexplained non-reader miss. For H2, compare the request and substantive judgement fields by question and day, since extra reader judgements can shift row IDs and the configuration checksum differs by arm. [Fixed-trajectory boundary](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:18) · [Filing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:256) · [Judgement propagation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:287)\n    18\t\n    19\t**Cost.** Repricing the **247** old-2 reader judgements from archived usage gives **$0.872 per sequence**, close to the design’s $0.86. On the fixed schedule, I counted **19 extra judgement days per linked sequence** even if every fresh reader marks its conclusion changed. That is about **8%** more accepted judgements. The $6.8 figure is a planning estimate. Replace the 50% propagation assumption with the dry-run count and price prompt growth, retries, and live reservations separately before asking Alex for the ceiling. [Cost estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:81) · [Settlement formula](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:438) · [Reservation formula](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py:227)     1\t**NOT READY.** The paired arms and date cutoffs are sound. The reading rule can still pass or fail because of how forecasts are parsed, which dates enter the denominator, or where a sourced figure appears in the request. Those decisions need to be fixed before building the harness.\n     2\t\n     3\t### Arms and dry proof\n     4\t\n     5\tI verified the first affected judgements at seq **167, 274 and 542**. None of the four readers’ accepted request hashes crosses its retirement cutoff, and all four stored refusals belong to non-readers. Retiring by reader and judgement date is sound for this archive. The proof should check the archive hit **before** transport as well as the outbound allow-list. A cache hit bypasses transport, so the allow-list alone cannot prove every post-link reader answer was fresh. Bind admission to the replay’s judgement day and require fresh answers for judgements actually made on or after the cutoff. [Retirement and admission](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:24) · [Judge-day entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/replay.py:119) · [Archive lookup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:69)\n     6\t\n     7\tThe **latest accepted upstream conclusion** is the right invariant. At prompt construction, capture its judgement ID and compare the rendered date, reference, and forecast against that snapshot. Check a same-day upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer, and a refused answer followed by an accepted retry. Checking the final ledger or archive call order can pick a conclusion that the reader had yet to receive. [Dry proof](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:49) · [Dependency block](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:127) · [Prompt construction and retry order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:327)\n     8\t\n     9\t### Blocking changes to the reading rule\n    10\t\n    11\t1. **Make hand reading and coverage agree.** I reproduced the parser’s **11, 56, and 1** counts. The validation sample contains another false definite beyond old-3 August 26. On old-1 September 8, the parser takes **$414.3B** for total revenue, but the answer’s final forecast is **$414–421B**. The **$419B** segment forecast overlaps it. Under the proposed coverage rule, a clean sequence can also become inconclusive because only 15 unflagged pairs get hand-confirmed. Use the parser to order the work and hand-classify **every distinct answer pair**. Treat a misparse as a diagnostic and record the corrected class, or mark the pair unresolved. Count carried-forward dates from those classifications. [Validation trace](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/parser-validation.txt:35) · [Parser rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/contradictions.py:50) · [Hand-reading and coverage rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:62)\n    12\t2. **Use the same dates for each pair’s comparison.** Carry-forward and three paired improvements are suitable for a decision about this window. Calculate paired rates on the same jointly readable dates against the fixed **73-day** window. An 80% floor by itself can still hide enough contradictions to reverse a small improvement. If unresolved dates could reverse a pair’s result, call it inconclusive. Keep the two-day control floor as an explicit testability condition. Report conversions from definite contradiction to overlap separately from clear reconciliation. Wider ranges can reduce definite contradictions while leaving agreement unresolved. [Primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:69)\n    13\t3. **Trace H3 through the link to the outside source.** Old-2’s July 10 `v14` request contains Bloomberg’s **$393B** estimate in `e195`. The `v9` request has no $393B. Correctly using that externally sourced **company** figure through `v14` is part of the intended benefit. Follow the figure through the exact upstream or earlier judgement to its dated outside statement. Fail a figure supported only by a system forecast, or a company estimate presented as segment consensus. Use keywords to find candidates and specify which references are checked regardless of keywords. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:84) · [July source-use check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:13)\n    14\t4. **Resolve H1–H5 and verdict precedence.** H1’s threshold is usable as an explicit tolerance. A reader whose correction is also refused is a product failure and must have a defined outcome even though replay stops. For H2, an unexplained non-reader request or substantive output difference invalidates the fixed-trajectory comparison. For H4, keep the review of every `v14` answer, and make unresolved CPU overlap consequential when the unexplained uplift supplies the apparent reconciliation. H5’s tolerance of one extra material failure per pair could likewise let a single unsupported, carried-forward forecast drive P. Define the question-date unit and how reader disagreements are resolved. A material error that supplies the apparent gain must prevent a pass. Write the precedence explicitly for confirmed harm, an invalid or incomplete run, and a completed comparison that misses P. [Outcome and harm rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:71) · [Second-refusal path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:340)\n    15\t\n    16\t### Cost and time\n    17\t\n    18\tThe **$5.8** base estimate is consistent with three controls at $0.872 and three linked sequences at about $1.05. Keeping July’s latch limits live reservations to six calls across the six sequences. Dry runs can measure request sizes and exercise propagation schedules. Future output tokens, retries, and actual propagation remain estimates. Old-2’s stored reader calls had a median of **38.2 seconds**. At that rate, 266 serial calls take about **2.8 hours** before replay and retries, so I would plan around three hours. This does not block the build once the reading and replay contracts are settled. [Cost and time](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [July latch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:56)     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 3 fixes the latest-upstream proof and makes the parser a triage tool. Three gaps remain in §4. Uncertainty can become a failure, a linked double refusal can become inconclusive, and a forecast outside the H5 sample can still supply the apparent improvement.\n     2\t\n     3\t### Blocking changes\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Give P three outcomes per pair.** Suppose 60 dates are jointly readable, the control has five definite contradictions and the linked arm has zero. On the other 13 dates, the control is clear and the linked forecast is unresolved. Both improvement and no improvement are possible. The current rule returns **fail**. Bound the difference in definite days over all 73 dates in both directions, retaining known classes on dates where only one arm is readable. Call a pair improved when even the least favourable completion improves, failed when even the most favourable completion cannot improve, and inconclusive when the unresolved dates could change the result. State explicitly whether the two-day control floor uses jointly readable dates. [Primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:78) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:109)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Separate product failure from infrastructure failure.** H1 calls a linked double refusal harm, but verdict step 1 makes any stop inconclusive. Production returns `judgement_refused_twice` and stops the day, so both rules apply to the same event. Make a confirmed linked double refusal fail and a control double refusal invalidate the comparison. Reserve the infrastructure outcome for budget, transport, and harness stops. For the rate check, count attempted reader judgements, including terminal refusals, and use matched reader-days for the paired rate. Report propagation-only refusals separately so extra clean judgement days cannot dilute the comparison. [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Product refusal path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/round.py:83)\n     8\t\n     9\t3. **Make decisive gains traceable beyond the seven H5 dates.** Keep the seven-date sample for H5’s comparative threshold. Also review the arithmetic and sourcing of every distinct linked answer that supplies a gain needed to pass P. Otherwise, an unsupported `v9` forecast between sample dates can carry the verdict. Preserve the actual forecast class separately from its eligibility for credit. If an unsupported CPU increment can be quantified, show the comparison without it. If its size is unresolved and it could decide P, withhold the pass. For H3, trigger review when a claim’s **amount, scope, or attribution** changes. An unchanged $393B can shift from company consensus to segment consensus. Use the paired control’s accepted conclusion in force on that date when it has no fresh judgement, and compare the specific grounding error. [H3–H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:93) · [July arithmetic and sourcing rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:5)\n    10\t\n    11\t### Thresholds and reading load\n    12\t\n    13\t- **60 of 73** is defensible as a coverage policy. It is **82.2%**, while the previous 80% rule would require **59** dates. State that 60 deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable dates. The two-sided bounds above should determine whether those missing dates matter.\n    14\t- **H1’s 5 points and 3 refusals** need an explicit tolerance rationale. With 266 linked judgements and zero control refusals, 13 linked refusals would still clear the gate. At this workload, the five-point rule already implies more than three additional refusals. **H5’s two extra failures** is a defensible tolerance for the fixed 14 question-dates per sequence once a single material error cannot supply the primary gain. Both are product tolerances to freeze before seeing results. [Thresholds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Workload basis](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:118)\n    15\t\n    16\tI counted **72 distinct v9/v14 answer pairs** on old-2’s 73 dates. Budget up to **438 pair classifications** across six sequences, about **213 linked v14 reviews** on the stored schedule, and **168 first and second H5 marks** before adjudication. The workload is feasible, but the protocol is incomplete. Before launch, pin a worksheet keyed by judgement IDs and date spans. Give readers the full forecast text, the endpoints and excerpt to record, and the exact request and dated sources for harm checks. Apply source-backed adjudication to verdict-changing disagreements in P, H3 and H4 as well as H5. The old-1 September 8 case, **$419B against $414–421B**, is a useful worked example of overlap. The roughly three-hour estimate covers the model run. Reader time needs its own estimate. [Hand-reading rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:68) · [Overlap example](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/parser-validation.txt:35) · [Run time](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:126)     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 4 fixes the handling of unresolved dates and linked double refusals. The remaining blocker is how P gives or withdraws credit for a gain. Two readers can apply the current wording to the same forecasts and reach opposite verdicts. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n     2\t\n     3\t### Blocking changes\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Review every credited gain in a provisionally improved pair.** “A gain needed for improved” leaves the review set open. If the control has two definite dates and the linked arm has zero, withdrawing either gain alone still leaves an improvement. Withdrawing both produces a tie. A reader who tests whether each gain is individually necessary could skip both. Require Rule B review of both supplying forecasts for every credited gain, deduplicated by judgement ID, then recompute P. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [July Rule B](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:8)\n     6\t2. **Use one rule for withdrawing credit.** H5 currently turns affected linked overlap or clear dates into definite dates, even where the control was also clear. A material error shared by both arms could therefore make linked P fail without any difference on those dates. Limit P adjustments to actual gains. For H4, say explicitly that removing a quantified unsupported CPU increment determines the **credit-adjusted** class. If the increment cannot be quantified and could decide the verdict, use the unresolved bounds. Keep the original forecast class, and keep confirmed double counting as harm. The fixed H5 sample still supplies its separate paired harm count. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n     7\t\n     8\t### Round-24 findings that hold as fixed\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Bounds and precedence.** The two-sided bounds preserve known classes on all 73 dates. The round-24 example with 13 unresolved linked dates now returns undecided. The two-day floor uses known control dates, and **60 resolved dates per arm** is an explicit coverage policy. A linked double refusal now fails before the generic stop rule. H1 uses matched reader-days, so propagation-only days cannot dilute it. The written threshold tolerates **exactly three** excess refusals and fails at four. Align the rationale with that boundary. [Bounds and coverage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:95) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:123)\n    11\t- **Sourcing and worksheet.** H3 now catches a changed scope or attribution at the same dollar amount and compares the specific error with the control conclusion in force that day. The worksheet specifies the judgement IDs, date spans, forecast excerpts, source traces, and adjudication. The $419B versus $414–421B example gives readers a concrete overlap case. In the dry review, count the second reader’s work separately from the number of distinct rows, and include H3 and the full gain review in the workload estimate. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:105) · [Worksheet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:128)\n    12\t\n    13\tSettle the two credit rules before building. The harness review can then use the two examples above as $0 verdict checks.     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 5 fixes both round-25 counterexamples. One ambiguity remains in how the credit rule handles multiple unquantifiable CPU gains. Two readers can still reach **pass** and **inconclusive** from the same answers. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89)\n     2\t\n     3\t### Round-25 findings\n     4\t\n     5\tBoth fixes hold. Every gain in a provisionally improved pair now gets Rule B review of both supplying judgements. If the control has two definite dates and both linked gains lose credit, the result is a tie. Only gain dates can be adjusted, so a material error shared by both arms on a clear date leaves P unchanged. H4 double counting and the H5 sample retain their separate harm checks. Both verdict checks are specified in §7. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:119) · [Verdict checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:178)\n     6\t\n     7\tH1 now explicitly tolerates three excess refusals and fails at four. The worksheet counts the two reads separately and includes H3 and the full gain review. Those findings are closed. [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [Reading load](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:131)\n     8\t\n     9\t### Exact remaining change\n    10\t\n    11\tRemove the per-gain condition **“and could decide the pair.”** Suppose a control has two definite dates and the linked arm has none, with both gains resting on unquantifiable unsupported CPU increments. Marking either gain unresolved by itself still leaves an improvement, since **1 < 2**. Marking both unresolved makes the pair **undecided**, since **2 < 2** is false. Mark **every** such gain unresolved, then recompute the bounds once across the pair. [Bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:80) · [CPU credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:93)\n    12\t\n    13\tState that the **60-date coverage check uses the original hand-read classes**, while the bounds use the credit-adjusted classes. A readable forecast with unsupported arithmetic still counts as readable, and its uncertainty still affects P. Add the two-unquantifiable-gains case to the $0 verdict checks. [Coverage rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:126)     1\t**READY TO BUILD THE PAID HARNESS.** Both round-26 findings are fixed. I found no remaining ambiguity in the revised credit and coverage rules that would let the same adjudicated answers produce opposite verdicts.\n     2\t\n     3\t- **Unquantifiable CPU gains.** Mark every affected gain unresolved, then recompute the pair’s bounds once. With two definite control dates and two unquantifiable linked gains, the linked count can range from zero to two, so the pair is **undecided**. §7 records this as the third $0 verdict check. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:91) · [Verdict checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:183)\n     4\t- **Coverage.** The 60-date floor uses the original hand-read classes. The bounds use the credit-adjusted classes. I checked the boundary in memory: with 60 readable linked dates, marking one gain unresolved for credit leaves coverage at 60 and increases uncertainty in P. [Coverage and bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:81)\n     5\t\n     6\tUse §4 as the verdict authority in the build. §2’s “any stop” shorthand should point to §4, which explicitly makes a linked double refusal a failure. The harness, worksheet builder, dry runs, and all three verdict checks return for review before any send. [Harness rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:40) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:129)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba paid_run.py | sed -n '230,402p'; nl -ba price.log | tail -21\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba paid_run.py | sed -n '230,402p'; nl -ba price.log | tail -21\"","aggregated_output":"   230\t                self.snapshot_checks += 1\n   231\t\n   232\t    def judge_day(self, original):\n   233\t        def wrapped(conn, archive_, cfg_, day, seq, **keywords):\n   234\t            before = archive_.conn.execute('select coalesce(max(call_id), 0) from model_calls').fetchone()[0]\n   235\t            self.day = day\n   236\t            try:\n   237\t                result = original(conn, archive_, cfg_, day, seq, **keywords)\n   238\t            finally:\n   239\t                self.day = None\n   240\t                after = archive_.conn.execute('select coalesce(max(call_id), 0) from model_calls').fetchone()[0]\n   241\t                self.day_windows[day] = (before, after)\n   242\t            failed = getattr(result, 'failed', None)\n   243\t            if failed and self.terminal is None:\n   244\t                text = str(failed)\n   245\t                view = text.split('judgement refused twice: ', 1)[1].split(':', 1)[0] if 'judgement refused twice: ' in text else None\n   246\t                self.terminal = ({'outcome': 'double refusal', 'view': view, 'day': day, 'reader': view in self.reader_days}\n   247\t                                 if view else {'outcome': 'infrastructure stop', 'day': day, 'reason': text[:300]})\n   248\t            return result\n   249\t        return wrapped\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\tdef prepare(folder, kind, draw):\n   253\t    if folder.exists():\n   254\t        raise SystemExit(f'{folder} exists: a sequence starts from an empty folder and is never resumed')\n   255\t    folder.mkdir(parents=True)\n   256\t    shutil.copyfile(OLD2 / 'archive.sqlite', folder / 'archive.sqlite')\n   257\t    archive = model.Archive(str(folder / 'archive.sqlite'))\n   258\t    top = archive.conn.execute('select coalesce(max(call_id), 0) from model_calls').fetchone()[0]\n   259\t    retired = []\n   260\t    if kind == 'control':\n   261\t        for reader, calls in retired_rows(readers(draw)).items():\n   262\t            for call_id in calls:\n   263\t                archive.retire(call_id)\n   264\t                retired.append((reader, call_id))\n   265\t    views = folder / 'views.sqlite'\n   266\t    marker = sqlite3.connect(views)\n   267\t    marker.execute('create table harness_only (name text, day text)')\n   268\t    marker.execute(\"insert into harness_only values ('paid_run.py', '2026-11-21')\")\n   269\t    marker.commit()\n   270\t    marker.close()\n   271\t    return archive, top, retired\n   272\t\n   273\t\n   274\tdef trajectory_check(views, reader_set):\n   275\t    \"\"\"H2: every non-reader judgement equal to old-2's by question and day (the request and every substantive field); every other table\n   276\t    equal, typed keys included.\"\"\"\n   277\t    built, stored = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{views}?mode=ro', uri=True), old2()\n   278\t    fields = ('view_id, publication_day, rung, reasons, reference, implied_outcome, cited_evidence_json, cited_views_json, pushes_json, '\n   279\t              'theme_pushes_json, changed, why_changed, request_sha256')\n   280\t\n   281\t    def rows(conn):\n   282\t        return sorted(tuple(r) for r in conn.execute(f'select {fields} from judgements') if r[0] not in reader_set)\n   283\t    tables = {'non-reader judgements': rows(built) == rows(stored)}\n   284\t    for name, sql in {'views': 'select view_id, question, created_seq from views',\n   285\t                      'view_keys': 'select view_id, measure, scope, period_start, period_end, unit from view_keys',\n   286\t                      'evidence': 'select evidence_id, quote, created_seq from evidence',\n   287\t                      'evidence_links': 'select evidence_id, view_id, seq from evidence_links',\n   288\t                      'rungs': 'select view_id, position, label, condition, lo, hi from rungs',\n   289\t                      'view_switches': 'select view_id, open, reason, after_seq from view_switches',\n   290\t                      'successions': 'select view_id, succeeds, seq from successions'}.items():\n   291\t        tables[name] = sorted(tuple(r) for r in built.execute(sql)) == sorted(tuple(r) for r in stored.execute(sql))\n   292\t    return {f'{k} equal': v for k, v in tables.items()}\n   293\t\n   294\t\n   295\tdef run_sequence(out, kind, draw, inner, ledger_state, transport_kind):\n   296\t    if kind not in ('linked', 'control') or draw not in DRAWS:\n   297\t        raise SystemExit('kind must be linked or control, draw 1, 2 or 3')\n   298\t    pinned = pinned_plan(draw)\n   299\t    folder = out / f'{kind}-{draw}'\n   300\t    archive, top, retired = prepare(folder, kind, draw)\n   301\t    run = SequenceRun(kind, draw, inner, folder)\n   302\t    cfg = config.load(str(V120 if kind == 'linked' else V118))\n   303\t    summary = {'kind': kind, 'draw': draw, 'transport': transport_kind, 'config': cfg.version, 'archive_top': top,\n   304\t               'reader days': run.reader_days, 'retired rows': len(retired), 'stopped': ''}\n   305\t    original_judge_day, original_build = round_module.judge_day, judging.build_prompt\n   306\t    round_module.judge_day, judging.build_prompt = run.judge_day(original_judge_day), run.build_prompt(original_build)\n   307\t    held = live_lock.acquire(str(folder / 'views.sqlite'))\n   308\t    try:\n   309\t        replay.run(testdb_path=str(ITEMS), views_path=str(folder / 'views.sqlite'), archive=archive, cfg=cfg, universe=universe.load(str(UNIVERSE)),\n   310\t                   from_day=WINDOW[0], until_day=WINDOW[1], transport=run.transport, log=lambda *a: None, only_objects={'NVDA'})\n   311\t    except Exception as error:\n   312\t        summary['stopped'] = f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'[:500]\n   313\t        if run.terminal is None:\n   314\t            run.terminal = {'outcome': 'infrastructure stop', 'reason': summary['stopped']}\n   315\t    finally:\n   316\t        live_lock.release(held)\n   317\t        round_module.judge_day, judging.build_prompt = original_judge_day, original_build\n   318\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"views.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n   319\t    reader_judgements = [dict(zip(('view', 'day', 'call_id', 'judgement_id'), r)) for r in conn.execute(\n   320\t        'select view_id, publication_day, call_id, judgement_id from judgements where view_id in (%s) order by judgement_id'\n   321\t        % ','.join('?' * len(pinned['readers'])), tuple(pinned['readers']))]\n   322\t    for j in reader_judgements:\n   323\t        window = run.day_windows.get(j['day'], (None, None))\n   324\t        j['must be fresh'] = j['day'] >= pinned['readers'][j['view']]\n   325\t        j['fresh on its day'] = window[0] is not None and window[0] < j['call_id'] <= window[1]\n   326\t        j['from the archive'] = j['call_id'] <= top\n   327\t    archive_conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n   328\t    attempt_rows = []\n   329\t    for call_id, request_json, sha in archive_conn.execute(\"select call_id, request_json, request_sha256 from model_calls where purpose='judge' and call_id > ?\", (top,)):\n   330\t        view = july_test.judged_view(json.loads(request_json))\n   331\t        day = next((d for d, (b, a) in run.day_windows.items() if b < call_id <= a), None)\n   332\t        attempt_rows.append({'call_id': call_id, 'view': view, 'day': day, 'refused': ':refused:' in sha, 'reader': view in pinned['readers']})\n   333\t    committed = sorted(list(r) for r in conn.execute(\"select view_id, depends_on, direction, seq from dependencies where kind='reads'\"))\n   334\t    with open(folder / 'snapshots.jsonl', 'w') as handle:\n   335\t        for snapshot in run.snapshots:\n   336\t            handle.write(json.dumps({'view': snapshot['view'], 'day': snapshot['day'], 'reads': snapshot['reads']}) + '\\n')\n   337\t    summary.update({\n   338\t        'terminal': run.terminal or {'outcome': 'complete'},\n   339\t        'links committed': committed,\n   340\t        'link jobs consumed': sorted(run.link_jobs),\n   341\t        'judge attempts at the transport': run.attempts,\n   342\t        'archive judge rows written (dated)': attempt_rows,\n   343\t        'stored refusals given back': run.refusals_given,\n   344\t        'reader judgements': reader_judgements,\n   345\t        'snapshot problems': run.snapshot_problems, 'snapshot checks': run.snapshot_checks,\n   346\t        'trajectory (H2)': trajectory_check(folder / 'views.sqlite', set(pinned['readers'])),\n   347\t        'latch': run.latch.reason, 'ledger': ledger_state(),\n   348\t    })\n   349\t    summary['acceptance'] = acceptance(summary, pinned, kind)\n   350\t    (folder / 'summary.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   351\t    return summary\n   352\t\n   353\t\n   354\tdef acceptance(summary, pinned, kind):\n   355\t    \"\"\"The one gate for the dry runs and the paid send.\"\"\"\n   356\t    judgements = summary['reader judgements']\n   357\t    checks = {\n   358\t        'window completed, no stop': summary['stopped'] == '' and summary['terminal'] == {'outcome': 'complete'},\n   359\t        'committed graph equals the pinned one (labels included)': summary['links committed'] == (pinned['graph'] if kind == 'linked' else []),\n   360\t        'probe jobs consumed exactly the pinned set': summary['link jobs consumed'] == (pinned['probe jobs'] if kind == 'linked' else []),\n   361\t        'every post-link reader judgement fresh on its own day': all(j['fresh on its day'] for j in judgements if j['must be fresh']),\n   362\t        'every pre-link reader judgement from the archive': all(j['from the archive'] for j in judgements if not j['must be fresh']),\n   363\t        'reader judgements present': bool(judgements),\n   364\t        'upstream snapshot clean': not summary['snapshot problems'],\n   365\t        'upstream snapshot exercised (linked)': kind == 'control' or summary['snapshot checks'] > 0,\n   366\t        'fixed trajectory (H2)': all(summary['trajectory (H2)'].values()),\n   367\t        'ledger clean (not stopped, no overrun)': not (summary.get('ledger') or {}).get('stopped') and not (summary.get('ledger') or {}).get('overrun'),\n   368\t    }\n   369\t    return dict(checks, accepted=all(checks.values()))\n   370\t\n   371\t\n   372\tdef refusing_judge(label, refuse, twice=False):\n   373\t    \"\"\"July's marker judge, except that the first attempt of each (view, n) in `refuse` cites an evidence id it was not shown, so the\n   374\t    code refuses it; with `twice` the retry is refused the same way (a terminal refusal), else answered normally.\"\"\"\n   375\t    plain, attempts = july_test.fake_judge(label, {}), {}\n   376\t\n   377\t    def answer(provider, request):\n   378\t        text = request['messages'][-1]['content']\n   379\t        view_id = july_test.judged_view(request)\n   380\t        retry = 'Correct it and answer again' in text\n   381\t        if not retry:\n   382\t            attempts[view_id] = attempts.get(view_id, 0) + 1\n   383\t        if (view_id, attempts.get(view_id)) in refuse and (twice or not retry):\n   384\t            data = {'rung': 3, 'reference': f'REFUSED|{label}|{view_id}', 'implied_outcome': f'REFUSED|{label}|{view_id}', 'reasons': 'x',\n   385\t                    'cites': ['e999999'], 'cites_views': [], 'pushes': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': '', 'theme_pushes': []}\n   386\t            return {'choices': [{'message': {'content': json.dumps(data)}}], 'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 100, 'completion_tokens': 10}}\n   387\t        return plain(provider, request)\n   388\t    return answer\n   389\t\n   390\t\n   391\tdef dry(out, kind, draw, refuse=(), twice=False):\n   392\t    \"\"\"$0: the sequence with a fake judge answering every bought judgement with markers unique to the sequence, the question and the\n   393\t    attempt (every one says changed, so propagation is at its largest), through the same acceptance gate as the send.\"\"\"\n   394\t    judge = refusing_judge(f'{kind}-{draw}', set(refuse), twice) if refuse else july_test.fake_judge(f'{kind}-{draw}', {})\n   395\t    summary = run_sequence(out, kind, draw, july_test.dispatcher(judge, july_test.fake_jev(set()), []), lambda: {}, 'dry')\n   396\t    print(json.dumps({'sequence': f'{kind}-{draw}', 'terminal': summary['terminal'], 'stopped': summary['stopped'][:200],\n   397\t                      'bought (admitted attempts)': sum(1 for a in summary['judge attempts at the transport'] if a['admitted']),\n   398\t                      'snapshot checks': summary['snapshot checks'], **summary['acceptance']}, indent=1))\n   399\t    return summary\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\tdef send(out, kind, draw, ledger_path, approved):\n    53\t    \"v9\": 70,\n    54\t    \"v16\": 56,\n    55\t    \"v21\": 50\n    56\t   },\n    57\t   \"estimate\": 0.906\n    58\t  },\n    59\t  \"control-3\": {\n    60\t   \"bought\": 247,\n    61\t   \"per reader\": {\n    62\t    \"v14\": 71,\n    63\t    \"v9\": 70,\n    64\t    \"v16\": 56,\n    65\t    \"v21\": 50\n    66\t   },\n    67\t   \"estimate\": 0.906\n    68\t  }\n    69\t },\n    70\t \"total estimate (linked +10% prompt growth, +5% retries)\": 5.89,\n    71\t \"largest single reservation\": 0.043,\n    72\t \"live reservations (one per sequence)\": 0.258\n    73\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba worksheet.py | sed -n '20,80p;137,255p'; nl -ba verdict.py | sed -n '15,92p;126,269p'; nl -ba ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10505,10520p;10611,10631p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba worksheet.py | sed -n '20,80p;137,255p'; nl -ba verdict.py | sed -n '15,92p;126,269p'; nl -ba ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10505,10520p;10611,10631p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\timport datetime\n    21\timport json\n    22\timport random\n    23\timport re\n    24\timport sqlite3\n    25\timport sys\n    26\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    27\t\n    28\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    29\timport contradictions                                                    # noqa: E402\n    30\t\n    31\tSEQUENCES = ('linked-1', 'linked-2', 'linked-3', 'control-1', 'control-2', 'control-3')\n    32\tSPAN = ('2026-07-10', '2026-09-20')\n    33\tFIXED_DATES = ('2026-07-12', '2026-07-26', '2026-08-09', '2026-08-23', '2026-09-06', '2026-09-13', '2026-09-20')\n    34\tSEGMENT, COMPANY, CPU = 'v9', 'v14', 'v8'\n    35\tREADERS = ('v9', 'v14', 'v16', 'v21')\n    36\tORDER = {'definite': 0, 'ambiguous': 1, 'overlap': 2, 'unparsed': 3, 'clear': 4}\n    37\tMARKET = re.compile(r'consensus|guidance|analyst|market expect', re.I)\n    38\tREADER_P = {'segment low': None, 'segment high': None, 'segment excerpt': '', 'company low': None, 'company high': None,\n    39\t            'company excerpt': '', 'class': None, 'note': ''}\n    40\tREADER_H = {'finding': None, 'error type': None, 'excerpt': '', 'source traced': '', 'note': ''}   # findings by part: verdict.H_FINDINGS\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\tdef window_dates():\n    44\t    day, end = datetime.date.fromisoformat(SPAN[0]), datetime.date.fromisoformat(SPAN[1])\n    45\t    while day <= end:\n    46\t        yield day.isoformat()\n    47\t        day += datetime.timedelta(days=1)\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\tdef connect(path):\n    51\t    return sqlite3.connect(f'file:{path}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef judgements(conn, view):\n    55\t    return conn.execute('select judgement_id, publication_day, implied_outcome, reasons, reference, call_id from judgements where view_id=? '\n    56\t                        'order by judgement_id', (view,)).fetchall()\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\tdef in_force(rows, date):\n    60\t    held = [j for j in rows if j[1] <= date]\n    61\t    return held[-1] if held else None\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\tdef request_text(archive, call_id):\n    65\t    row = archive.execute('select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?', (call_id,)).fetchone()\n    66\t    return json.loads(row[0])['messages'][-1]['content'] if row else None\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\tdef snapshots(folder):\n    70\t    path = Path(folder) / 'snapshots.jsonl'\n    71\t    return [json.loads(line) for line in path.read_text().splitlines()] if path.exists() else []\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\tdef shown(snaps, view, day):\n    75\t    \"\"\"The read judgements a reader was shown for its judgement on `day` (the last prompt built for it that day).\"\"\"\n    76\t    built = [s for s in snaps if s['view'] == view and s['day'] == day]\n    77\t    return {target: (state or {}).get('judgement_id') for target, state in (built[-1]['reads'] if built else {}).items()}\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\tdef p_rows(folder, label):\n   137\tdef read_judgement_trace(conn, archive, read_ids):\n   138\t    \"\"\"For each read judgement a reader was shown: its id, comparison point, forecast and its own request (where its figures' dated\n   139\t    sources are), so a linked figure can be followed to its outside statement.\"\"\"\n   140\t    out = {}\n   141\t    for target, judgement_id in read_ids.items():\n   142\t        if judgement_id is None:\n   143\t            continue\n   144\t        row = conn.execute('select reference, implied_outcome, call_id from judgements where judgement_id=?', (judgement_id,)).fetchone()\n   145\t        if row:\n   146\t            out[target] = {'judgement': judgement_id, 'reference': row[0], 'forecast': row[1], 'request': request_text(archive, row[2])}\n   147\t    return out\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\tdef h_rows(out, name, label):\n   151\t    folder = Path(out) / name\n   152\t    conn, archive, snaps = connect(folder / 'views.sqlite'), connect(folder / 'archive.sqlite'), snapshots(folder)\n   153\t    reader_days = json.loads((folder / 'summary.json').read_text())['reader days']\n   154\t    pair = name.replace('linked', 'control') if name.startswith('linked') else name.replace('control', 'linked')\n   155\t    other = connect(Path(out) / pair / 'views.sqlite')\n   156\t    rows = []\n   157\t\n   158\t    def row(part, view, judgement, **extra):\n   159\t        read_ids = shown(snaps, view, judgement[1])\n   160\t        counterpart = in_force(judgements(other, view), judgement[1])\n   161\t        return {'part': part, 'sequence': name, 'view': view, 'judgement': judgement[0], 'day': judgement[1],\n   162\t                'forecast': judgement[2], 'reasons': judgement[3], 'reference': judgement[4],\n   163\t                'request': request_text(archive, judgement[5]), 'shown': read_ids, 'trace': read_judgement_trace(conn, archive, read_ids),\n   164\t                'paired arm in force': {'sequence': pair, 'judgement': counterpart[0], 'forecast': counterpart[2],\n   165\t                                        'reference': counterpart[4]} if counterpart else None,\n   166\t                **extra, 'reader': dict(READER_H)}\n   167\t    if name.startswith('linked'):\n   168\t        for j in judgements(conn, COMPANY):\n   169\t            if j[1] >= reader_days[COMPANY]:\n   170\t                rows.append(row('H4', COMPANY, j, cpu_shown=shown(snaps, COMPANY, j[1]).get(CPU)))\n   171\t    for view in (SEGMENT, COMPANY):\n   172\t        for date in FIXED_DATES:\n   173\t            j = in_force(judgements(conn, view), date)\n   174\t            if j:\n   175\t                rows.append(row('H5', view, j, fixed_date=date))\n   176\t    for view in READERS:\n   177\t        for j in judgements(conn, view):\n   178\t            if j[1] < reader_days.get(view, '9999'):\n   179\t                continue\n   180\t            flag = structural_flag(view, j[4], request_text(archive, j[5]))\n   181\t            fixed = j[1] in FIXED_DATES\n   182\t            if flag or fixed:\n   183\t                rows.append(row('H3', view, j, structural_flag=flag, fixed_date=j[1] if fixed else None))\n   184\t        for date in FIXED_DATES:                     # the conclusion in force on each fixed date, when it was judged earlier\n   185\t            j = in_force(judgements(conn, view), date)\n   186\t            if j and j[1] >= reader_days.get(view, '9999') and j[1] != date and not any(r['part'] == 'H3' and r['judgement'] == j[0] for r in rows):\n   187\t                rows.append(row('H3', view, j, structural_flag=structural_flag(view, j[4], request_text(archive, j[5])), fixed_date=date))\n   188\t    return rows\n   189\t\n   190\t\n   191\tdef build(out, target, key_path, seed=None):\n   192\t    \"\"\"Two packets: <target> with `.p.json` (the blind P rows only, shuffled labels) and `.h.json` (the H rows, which reveal the arm);\n   193\t    the key goes to <key_path>, outside the repository.\"\"\"\n   194\t    labels = [f'S-{c}' for c in 'ABCDEF']\n   195\t    random.Random(seed).shuffle(labels) if seed is not None else random.SystemRandom().shuffle(labels)\n   196\t    key = dict(zip(labels, SEQUENCES))\n   197\t    p_packet, h_packet = [], []\n   198\t    for label, name in key.items():\n   199\t        p_packet += p_rows(Path(out) / name, label)\n   200\t        h_packet += h_rows(out, name, label)\n   201\t    random.Random(seed).shuffle(p_packet) if seed is not None else random.SystemRandom().shuffle(p_packet)\n   202\t    p_packet.sort(key=lambda r: ORDER.get(r['lead class'], 9))\n   203\t    h_packet.sort(key=lambda r: (r['part'], r['sequence'], r['day']))\n   204\t    Path(f'{target}.p.json').write_text(json.dumps(p_packet, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   205\t    Path(f'{target}.h.json').write_text(json.dumps(h_packet, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   206\t    Path(key_path).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n   207\t    Path(key_path).write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   208\t    summary = {'P': len(p_packet), **{part: sum(1 for r in h_packet if r['part'] == part) for part in ('H3', 'H4', 'H5')},\n   209\t               'H3 structural flags': sum(1 for r in h_packet if r['part'] == 'H3' and r.get('structural_flag')),\n   210\t               'P dates per label': {label: sum(len(r['dates']) for r in p_packet if r['sequence'] == label) for label in key}}\n   211\t    print(json.dumps(summary))\n   212\t    return p_packet, h_packet\n   213\t\n   214\t\n   215\tdef gains(out, read_sheet, key_path, target):\n   216\t    \"\"\"The gain review rows: for every date a linked sequence's pair is read overlap or clear while its control's is read definite, the\n   217\t    two linked answers in force (v9's and v14's), deduplicated by judgement id, each with its exact request, for rule B and H4.\"\"\"\n   218\t    key = json.loads(Path(key_path).read_text())\n   219\t    by_name = {name: label for label, name in key.items()}\n   220\t    sheet = json.loads(Path(read_sheet).read_text())\n   221\t    classes = {}\n   222\t    for row in sheet:\n   223\t        if row['part'] == 'P':\n   224\t            for date in row['dates']:\n   225\t                classes[(key[row['sequence']], date)] = (row['reader']['class'], row['segment judgement'], row['company judgement'])\n   226\t    rows = []\n   227\t    for n in ('1', '2', '3'):\n   228\t        archive = connect(Path(out) / f'linked-{n}' / 'archive.sqlite')\n   229\t        conn = connect(Path(out) / f'linked-{n}' / 'views.sqlite')\n   230\t        gain_dates = {}\n   231\t        for date in window_dates():\n   232\t            linked, control = classes.get((f'linked-{n}', date)), classes.get((f'control-{n}', date))\n   233\t            if linked and control and linked[0] in ('overlap', 'clear') and control[0] == 'definite':\n   234\t                for judgement_id in linked[1:]:\n   235\t                    gain_dates.setdefault(judgement_id, []).append(date)\n   236\t        for judgement_id, dates in sorted(gain_dates.items()):\n   237\t            j = conn.execute('select judgement_id, view_id, publication_day, implied_outcome, reasons, reference, call_id from judgements '\n   238\t                             'where judgement_id=?', (judgement_id,)).fetchone()\n   239\t            rows.append({'part': 'gain', 'pair': n, 'judgement': j[0], 'view': j[1], 'day': j[2],\n   240\t                         'forecast': j[3], 'reasons': j[4], 'reference': j[5], 'request': request_text(archive, j[6]), 'gain dates': dates,\n   241\t                         'reader': {'rule B material failure': None, 'reconciliation rests on it': None, 'cpu increment': None,\n   242\t                                    'credit-adjusted class': None, 'note': ''}})\n   243\t    Path(target).write_text(json.dumps(rows, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   244\t    print(json.dumps({'gain review rows': len(rows)}))\n   245\t    return rows\n   246\t\n   247\t\n   248\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   249\t    if sys.argv[1] == 'build':\n   250\t        build(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   251\t    elif sys.argv[1] == 'gains':\n   252\t        gains(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4], sys.argv[5])\n   253\t    else:\n   254\t        raise SystemExit('build or gains')\n    15\t\"\"\"\n    16\timport datetime\n    17\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    18\timport json\n    19\timport sys\n    20\t\n    21\tWINDOW_DATES = 73\n    22\tSPAN = ('2026-07-10', '2026-09-20')\n    23\tCLASSES = ('definite', 'overlap', 'clear', 'unresolved')\n    24\tFLAGS = ('linked_double_refusal', 'infrastructure_stop', 'control_double_refusal', 'trajectory_difference', 'h1', 'h3', 'h4_double_count', 'h5')\n    25\tSEQUENCES = ('linked-1', 'linked-2', 'linked-3', 'control-1', 'control-2', 'control-3')\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\tclass Refused(SystemExit):\n    29\t    pass\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\tdef calendar():\n    33\t    day, end = datetime.date.fromisoformat(SPAN[0]), datetime.date.fromisoformat(SPAN[1])\n    34\t    out = []\n    35\t    while day <= end:\n    36\t        out.append(day.isoformat())\n    37\t        day += datetime.timedelta(days=1)\n    38\t    return out\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef read_classes(rows, key):\n    42\t    \"\"\"{sequence: {date: class}}, refusing anything but exactly one valid class per window date per sequence.\"\"\"\n    43\t    window = calendar()\n    44\t    out = {name: {} for name in SEQUENCES}\n    45\t    for row in rows:\n    46\t        if row.get('part') != 'P':\n    47\t            continue\n    48\t        name = key.get(row['sequence'])\n    49\t        if name not in out:\n    50\t            raise Refused(f'unknown sequence label {row[\"sequence\"]!r}')\n    51\t        value = row['reader']['class']\n    52\t        if value not in CLASSES:\n    53\t            raise Refused(f'{row[\"sequence\"]}: class {value!r} is not one of {CLASSES}')\n    54\t        for date in row['dates']:\n    55\t            if date not in window:\n    56\t                raise Refused(f'{row[\"sequence\"]}: date {date} is outside the window')\n    57\t            if date in out[name]:\n    58\t                raise Refused(f'{row[\"sequence\"]}: date {date} read twice')\n    59\t            out[name][date] = value\n    60\t    for name, classes in out.items():\n    61\t        if sorted(classes) != window:\n    62\t            raise Refused(f'{name}: {len(classes)} dates read, not the window\\'s {len(window)}')\n    63\t    return out\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\tdef summary_flags(out):\n    67\t    summaries = {name: json.loads((Path(out) / name / 'summary.json').read_text()) for name in SEQUENCES}\n    68\t\n    69\t    def terminal(name):\n    70\t        return summaries[name]['terminal'].get('outcome')\n    71\t    return {'linked_double_refusal': any(terminal(n) == 'double refusal' for n in SEQUENCES if n.startswith('linked')),\n    72\t            'control_double_refusal': any(terminal(n) == 'double refusal' for n in SEQUENCES if n.startswith('control')),\n    73\t            'infrastructure_stop': any(terminal(n) == 'infrastructure stop' for n in SEQUENCES),\n    74\t            'trajectory_difference': any(not all(s['trajectory (H2)'].values()) for s in summaries.values())}\n    75\tREADABLE = 60\n    76\tCONTROL_FLOOR = 2\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\tdef date_classes(rows, sequence):\n    80\t    out = {}\n    81\t    for row in rows:\n    82\t        if row['sequence'] == sequence:\n    83\t            for date in row['dates']:\n    84\t                out[date] = row['reader']['class']\n    85\t    return out\n    86\t\n    87\t\n    88\tdef counts(classes, all_dates):\n    89\t    d = sum(1 for date in all_dates if classes.get(date) == 'definite')\n    90\t    u = sum(1 for date in all_dates if classes.get(date) in (None, 'unresolved'))\n    91\t    return d, u\n    92\t\n   126\t    if 'untestable' in outcomes:\n   127\t        return 'inconclusive', 'the class did not recur in a control'\n   128\t    if all(o == 'improved' for o in outcomes):\n   129\t        return 'pass', 'all three pairs improved'\n   130\t    if 'not improved' in outcomes:\n   131\t        return 'fail', 'a pair not improved'\n   132\t    return 'inconclusive', 'a pair undecided'\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\tH_FINDINGS = {'H3': ('ok', 'grounding error'), 'H4': ('ok', 'double count', 'supported increment', 'unresolved overlap'),\n   136\t              'H5': ('pass', 'material failure')}\n   137\tERROR_TYPES = ('own figure as market', 'company as segment consensus')\n   138\tH1_EXCESS = 4\n   139\tH5_EXCESS = 2\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\tdef h_key(row):\n   143\t    return (row['part'], row['sequence'], row['view'], row['judgement'], row.get('fixed_date'))\n   144\t\n   145\t\n   146\tdef h1(summaries):\n   147\t    \"\"\"Per pair: model refusals of readers on matched reader-days (judged in both arms), linked minus control; harm at 4 or more.\"\"\"\n   148\t    harm = {}\n   149\t    for n in ('1', '2', '3'):\n   150\t        linked, control = summaries[f'linked-{n}'], summaries[f'control-{n}']\n   151\t        days = {(j['view'], j['day']) for j in linked['reader judgements']} & {(j['view'], j['day']) for j in control['reader judgements']}\n   152\t\n   153\t        def refusals(summary):\n   154\t            return sum(1 for r in summary['archive judge rows written (dated)'] if r['reader'] and r['refused'] and (r['view'], r['day']) in days)\n   155\t        harm[n] = refusals(linked) - refusals(control) >= H1_EXCESS\n   156\t    return harm\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\tdef h_flags(h_rows):\n   160\t    \"\"\"H3, H4 and H5 from the read H rows.\"\"\"\n   161\t    by_key = {h_key(r): r for r in h_rows}\n   162\t    h3 = False\n   163\t    for row in h_rows:\n   164\t        if row['part'] == 'H3' and row['sequence'].startswith('linked') and row['reader']['finding'] == 'grounding error':\n   165\t            paired = row.get('paired arm in force') or {}\n   166\t            matched = [r for r in h_rows if r['part'] == 'H3' and r['sequence'] == paired.get('sequence') and r['judgement'] == paired.get('judgement')\n   167\t                       and r['reader']['finding'] == 'grounding error' and r['reader']['error type'] == row['reader']['error type']]\n   168\t            if not matched:\n   169\t                h3 = True\n   170\t    h4 = any(r['part'] == 'H4' and r['reader']['finding'] == 'double count' for r in h_rows)\n   171\t    h5 = False\n   172\t    for n in ('1', '2', '3'):\n   173\t        def failures(name):\n   174\t            return sum(1 for r in h_rows if r['part'] == 'H5' and r['sequence'] == name and r['reader']['finding'] == 'material failure')\n   175\t        if failures(f'linked-{n}') - failures(f'control-{n}') >= H5_EXCESS:\n   176\t            h5 = True\n   177\t    return {'h3': h3, 'h4_double_count': h4, 'h5': h5}, by_key\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\tdef gain_credit(gain_rows, gain_dates):\n   181\t    \"\"\"{date: adjustment} from the reviews of the two supplying linked answers of each gain: a material rule-B failure on which the\n   182\t    reconciliation rests withdraws credit ('none'); an unquantifiable CPU increment makes the date unresolved; a quantified one gives the\n   183\t    credit-adjusted class.\"\"\"\n   184\t    by_judgement = {r['judgement']: r for r in gain_rows}\n   185\t    credit = {}\n   186\t    for date, judgement_ids in gain_dates.items():\n   187\t        reviews = [by_judgement.get(j) for j in judgement_ids]\n   188\t        if any(r is None for r in reviews):\n   189\t            raise Refused(f'{date}: a supplying answer has no gain review')\n   190\t        for r in reviews:\n   191\t            fields = r['reader']\n   192\t            if not isinstance(fields.get('rule B material failure'), bool) or fields.get('cpu increment') not in ('none', 'quantified', 'unquantifiable'):\n   193\t                raise Refused(f'gain review of judgement {r[\"judgement\"]} is incomplete')\n   194\t        if any(r['reader']['rule B material failure'] and r['reader'].get('reconciliation rests on it') for r in reviews):\n   195\t            credit[date] = 'none'\n   196\t        elif any(r['reader']['cpu increment'] == 'unquantifiable' for r in reviews):\n   197\t            credit[date] = 'unresolved'\n   198\t        else:\n   199\t            quantified = [r['reader'].get('credit-adjusted class') for r in reviews if r['reader']['cpu increment'] == 'quantified']\n   200\t            if quantified:\n   201\t                if any(q not in CLASSES for q in quantified):\n   202\t                    raise Refused(f'{date}: a quantified CPU increment without a credit-adjusted class')\n   203\t                credit[date] = 'definite' if 'definite' in quantified else quantified[0]\n   204\t    return credit\n   205\t\n   206\t\n   207\tdef main(out, p_path, h_path, gains_path, key_path):\n   208\t    import worksheet\n   209\t    summaries = {name: json.loads((Path(out) / name / 'summary.json').read_text()) for name in SEQUENCES}\n   210\t    flags = summary_flags(out)\n   211\t    if flags['linked_double_refusal']:                          # precedence (1), before any worksheet is required\n   212\t        print(json.dumps({'verdict': ('fail', 'a linked double refusal')}))\n   213\t        return 'fail', 'a linked double refusal'\n   214\t    if flags['infrastructure_stop'] or flags['control_double_refusal'] or flags['trajectory_difference'] or \\\n   215\t            not all(s['acceptance']['accepted'] for s in summaries.values()):\n   216\t        print(json.dumps({'verdict': ('inconclusive', 'invalid or incomplete'), 'flags': flags,\n   217\t                          'rejected': [n for n, s in summaries.items() if not s['acceptance']['accepted']]}))\n   218\t        return 'inconclusive', 'invalid or incomplete'\n   219\t    key = json.loads(Path(key_path).read_text())\n   220\t    classes = read_classes(json.loads(Path(p_path).read_text()), key)\n   221\t    h_rows = json.loads(Path(h_path).read_text())\n   222\t    expected = {h_key(r) for name in SEQUENCES for r in worksheet.h_rows(out, name, None)}\n   223\t    read = {h_key(r): r for r in h_rows}\n   224\t    if set(read) != expected:\n   225\t        raise Refused(f'the H packet holds {len(set(read) & expected)} of the {len(expected)} expected rows '\n   226\t                      f'({len(set(read) - expected)} unexpected)')\n   227\t    for k, row in read.items():\n   228\t        if row['reader']['finding'] not in H_FINDINGS[row['part']]:\n   229\t            raise Refused(f'{k}: finding {row[\"reader\"][\"finding\"]!r} is not one of {H_FINDINGS[row[\"part\"]]}')\n   230\t        if row['part'] == 'H3' and row['reader']['finding'] == 'grounding error' and row['reader']['error type'] not in ERROR_TYPES:\n   231\t            raise Refused(f'{k}: a grounding error needs its type, one of {ERROR_TYPES}')\n   232\t    derived, _ = h_flags(h_rows)\n   233\t    flags.update(derived)\n   234\t    flags['h1'] = any(h1(summaries).values())\n   235\t    gain_rows = json.loads(Path(gains_path).read_text())\n   236\t    window = calendar()\n   237\t    outcomes, details = [], {}\n   238\t    for n in ('1', '2', '3'):\n   239\t        linked, control = classes[f'linked-{n}'], classes[f'control-{n}']\n   240\t        provisional = pair_outcome(linked, control, {}, window)[0]\n   241\t        credit = {}\n   242\t        if provisional == 'improved':\n   243\t            p_rows = [r for r in json.loads(Path(p_path).read_text()) if key[r['sequence']] == f'linked-{n}']\n   244\t            supplying = {d: (r['segment judgement'], r['company judgement']) for r in p_rows for d in r['dates']}\n   245\t            gain_dates = {d: supplying[d] for d in window if linked[d] in ('overlap', 'clear') and control[d] == 'definite'}\n   246\t            credit = gain_credit([g for g in gain_rows if g['pair'] == n], gain_dates)\n   247\t        outcome, detail = pair_outcome(linked, control, credit, window)\n   248\t        outcomes.append(outcome)\n   249\t        details[n] = dict(detail, outcome=outcome, provisional=provisional, adjusted=credit)\n   250\t    result = verdict(outcomes, flags)\n   251\t    print(json.dumps({'pairs': details, 'flags': flags, 'verdict': result}, indent=1))\n   252\t    return result\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\t# ---------- the $0 verdict checks ----------\n   256\t\n   257\tdef synthetic(definite_control=(), definite_linked=(), unresolved_linked=(), total=WINDOW_DATES):\n   258\t    dates = [f'd{i:02d}' for i in range(total)]\n   259\t    control = {d: ('definite' if d in definite_control else 'clear') for d in dates}\n   260\t    linked = {d: ('definite' if d in definite_linked else 'unresolved' if d in unresolved_linked else 'clear') for d in dates}\n   261\t    return dates, linked, control\n   262\t\n   263\t\n   264\tdef checks():\n   265\t    results = {}\n   266\t    dates, linked, control = synthetic(definite_control=('d01', 'd02'))\n   267\t    results['control 2 definite, linked none: improved'] = pair_outcome(linked, control, {}, dates)[0] == 'improved'\n   268\t    results['round 25: both gains reviewed and withdrawn: a tie, not improved'] = \\\n   269\t        pair_outcome(linked, control, {'d01': 'none', 'd02': 'none'}, dates)[0] == 'not improved'\n 10505\t## D699 · 2026-11-21 · Alex continues with A: the whole-window measurement of the linking version, designed with the council at $0 first\n 10506\t\n 10507\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21), replying to the next-step question after D698 (A, recommended: run poc-v119 over 07-01..09-20 once,\n 10508\t  about $9 as one poc-v118 run cost, its measurement designed with the council at $0 before any send: whether related number questions\n 10509\t  still contradict each other, which links the matcher chose and whether any is wrong, whether the self-made market reference becomes\n 10510\t  rarer, whether other questions get worse; then decide whether the new version replaces the product. B: stop, poc-v118 stays): \"continue\".\n 10511\t- **Decided (Alex):** A. The $0 design and its council come first. The ~$9 was an estimate, not a ceiling: the paid send waits for the\n 10512\t  council's pass and a ceiling Alex names. poc-v118 stays the product until a later decision.\n 10513\t- **Next:** the design (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/`), its $0 proofs, council rounds, then the ceiling question to Alex.\n 10514\t\n 10515\t## D700 · 2026-11-21 · Alex approves a $0.10 ceiling for the census of the link decisions before the whole-window run\n 10516\t\n 10517\t- **The question** (after council R round 13 found the July link's direction close to a coin flip, `docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md`\n 10518\t  §3): \"正式完整运行之前，我想先花几美分，单独测一下连题这一步本身 … 把整个期间会遇到的 105 对候选题，每对问 3 遍，看它连得稳不稳、有没有连错。这一步不付钱买任何判断，预计 2–7 美分。上限 10 美分可以吗？\"\n 10519\t  Options: 10 美分 (recommended; link questions only, three draws, stop above $0.10, the result goes to the council before the run is\n 10520\t  decided); 先别花 (no census; the run discussed with the known coin-flip risk).\n 10611\t## D707 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the paid design (version 6, rounds 22–27) for building its harness\n 10612\t\n 10613\t- **The design** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md`): three linked sequences (poc-v120, links from probe draws\n 10614\t  1–3, now written) paired with three fresh controls (poc-v118, the readers' old judgements retired by reader and day from each link's\n 10615\t  day) on old-2's trajectory; readers v9, v14, v16, v21; July's latch, six sequences at once; the primary line P counts, over the 73\n 10616\t  dates 07-10..09-20, the dates on which Data Center's FY2027 low end exceeds total revenue's high end, every distinct answer pair read\n 10617\t  by hand (the parser `contradictions.py` orders the work; `parser-validation.md`), three outcomes per pair from two-sided bounds, one\n 10618\t  credit rule for gains; harm lines H1–H5; verdict precedence; a pinned worksheet. The attainable verdict ($0): the stored v118 draws\n 10619\t  show 11, 56 and 1 definite days.\n 10620\t- **The council:** round 22 NOT READY (four changes), 23 (reading rule, freshness), 24 (three outcomes, product against infrastructure\n 10621\t  failure, decisive gains, worksheet), 25 (one credit rule), 26 (unquantifiable gains together, coverage), 27 READY TO BUILD THE PAID\n 10622\t  HARNESS. The harness, the worksheet builder, the dry runs and three $0 verdict checks return for review before any send; the send\n 10623\t  waits for a ceiling Alex names (estimate about $5.8 before retries and reservations).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"One pair can rule out the three-pair pass. The stop rule needs to distinguish a measured lack of improvement from a result the forecasts leave undecided."}}
